const recipes = [{"id": "hot-smoked-salmon-spinach-caper-pasta-bake", "title": "Hot-smoked salmon, spinach & caper pasta bake", "category": "Fish & seafood", "description": "Smoky salmon, sweet leeks and a crisp caper topping.", "lines": ["Serves: 4 Prep: 20-25 min Bake: 20-25 min", "Ingredients", "Pasta & filling", "250g dried fusilli, penne or macaroni; 350g plain hot-smoked salmon, skin and bones removed; 1 large leek,", "sliced; 1 small onion, diced; 100g baby spinach; 100g frozen peas (optional).", "Creamy sauce", "40g butter; 35g plain flour; 500ml whole milk (plus 50-75ml if needed); 100g full-fat cream cheese; 50g", "mature cheddar, grated; 1 tsp Dijon mustard; 1/2 tsp caraway seeds, lightly crushed; zest of 1/2 lemon; 1", "tsp lemon juice, then adjust to taste; 2 tbsp fresh dill, chopped; black pepper.", "Capers & topping", "1 tbsp capers, drained (reserve 1 tsp for topping); 50g panko breadcrumbs; 20g butter, melted; 25g mature", "cheddar, grated; 1 tbsp chopped parsley or dill.", "Method", "1. Prepare the pasta. Heat oven to 200°C conventional / 180°C fan. Cook the pasta in boiling water for 2", "minutes less than its packet time. Drain.", "2. Soften the vegetables. Melt 40g butter in a large saucepan over medium-low heat. Add the leek, onion", "and caraway; cook for 8-10 minutes until soft, without browning.", "3. Make the sauce. Stir in flour and cook for 1-2 minutes. Gradually whisk in milk; gently simmer for 3-5", "minutes until smooth and thickened.", "4. Add flavour and spinach. Take off the heat. Stir in cream cheese, 50g cheddar, Dijon, lemon zest, dill and", "black pepper. Roughly chop half the capers remaining after reserving 1 tsp for the topping; stir all those", "sauce capers in. Add spinach and stir until wilted, returning briefly to low heat if necessary.", "5. Fold everything together. Mix in pasta and optional peas. Fold in 350g hot-smoked salmon in large", "flakes. Stir in 1 tsp lemon juice, taste, and add more only if needed. The filling should be generously saucy:", "loosen with 50-75ml milk if necessary. Taste before adding salt.", "6. Make the crunchy topping. Combine panko, 20g melted butter, 25g cheddar, herbs and the reserved 1", "tsp finely chopped capers, patted dry. Spoon filling into a lightly buttered baking dish (about 25 x 20cm)", "and scatter topping evenly.", "7. Bake and rest. Bake 20-25 minutes until golden, bubbling and piping hot in the centre. Rest 5 minutes", "before serving.", "Tips & make-ahead", "• Use hot-smoked salmon, which is cooked and flaky, not thinly sliced cold-smoked salmon. Keep the salmon", "in large pieces when folding it in.", "• Drain and pat dry the capers for the topping so the panko stays crisp. The capers, cheese and salmon", "bring salt, so season only after tasting.", "• For a make-ahead bake, assemble without topping, cool promptly and refrigerate. Add topping just before", "baking; from chilled, allow around 30-35 minutes, until piping hot throughout.", "• Cool leftovers promptly, keep refrigerated and eat within 48 hours; reheat once until piping hot", "throughout. A sharp green salad or steamed broccoli makes a good accompaniment."], "pdf": "Hot_Smoked_Salmon_Spinach_Capers_Pasta_Bake.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["250g dried fusilli, penne or macaroni", "350g plain hot-smoked salmon, skin and bones removed", "1 large leek, sliced", "1 small onion, diced", "100g baby spinach", "100g frozen peas (optional)", "40g butter", "35g plain flour", "500ml whole milk (plus 50-75ml if needed)", "100g full-fat cream cheese", "50g mature cheddar, grated", "1 tsp Dijon mustard", "1/2 tsp caraway seeds, lightly crushed; zest of 1/2 lemon", "1 tsp lemon juice, then adjust to taste", "2 tbsp fresh dill, chopped", "black pepper", "1 tbsp capers, drained (reserve 1 tsp for topping)", "50g panko breadcrumbs", "20g butter, melted", "25g mature cheddar, grated", "1 tbsp chopped parsley or dill"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "20–25 min", "cook": "20–25 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "korean-slow-braised-beef-galbi-jjim", "title": "Korean slow-braised beef (galbi-jjim)", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Boneless beef with pear, soy, ginger, shiitake and daikon.", "lines": ["Galbi-jjim-inspired beef with pear, soy, ginger, shiitake and daikon | 4 generous servings | 20–25", "min prep | 3–4 hours oven (or 7–9 hours slow cooker)", "An easy, mostly hands-off Korean-inspired beef braise. Large chunks of beef become fork-tender in a", "sweet-savoury sauce; reduce the cooking liquid at the end for a glossy finish. Traditional galbi-jjim uses", "ribs; this boneless variation uses chuck or shin.", "Ingredients — beef and vegetables", "• 800g boneless beef chuck / braising steak (preferred), or beef shin, cut into 4–5cm chunks", "• 2 medium carrots, cut into large pieces", "• 250g mooli (daikon radish), cut into large chunks", "• 150g shiitake mushrooms, fresh or rehydrated dried", "• 1 medium onion, quartered", "• 3 spring onions, sliced, for serving", "Ingredients — braising sauce", "• 100ml regular soy sauce (not dark soy sauce)", "• 1 ripe pear, grated (a Conference pear is fine)", "• 5 garlic cloves, finely grated or minced", "• 20g fresh ginger, grated", "• 2 tbsp mirin", "• 1 tbsp brown sugar", "• 1 tbsp toasted sesame oil", "• 300ml water for oven method; only 100ml for slow cooker", "• ½ tsp freshly ground black pepper", "To serve", "Steamed short-grain rice, toasted sesame seeds and kimchi or another sharp pickle.", "Oven method — cast-iron casserole", "1. Mix the sauce. Combine soy sauce, grated pear, garlic, ginger, mirin, brown sugar, sesame oil and", "black pepper.", "2. Assemble. Trim large pieces of external fat from the beef, leaving marbling. Put beef and onion in a", "heavy lidded casserole. Pour over the sauce and 300ml water; stir. The meat need not be submerged.", "3. Braise. Bring just to a simmer on the hob, cover tightly, then transfer to an oven at 150°C", "conventional / 130°C fan for about 2 hours.", "4. Add vegetables. Stir in carrots and mooli. Cover and cook another 45–75 minutes. Add shiitake for the", "final 30 minutes. Continue until the beef yields easily to a fork; shin may need longer.", "5. Finish the glaze. Lift beef and vegetables out with a slotted spoon. Skim fat from the liquid and simmer", "it uncovered on the hob for 5–15 minutes until glossy and lightly thickened. Taste as it reduces; add", "water if too salty. Return beef and vegetables and coat gently.", "6. Serve. Finish with sliced spring onions and toasted sesame seeds. Serve with rice and kimchi.", "Winter comfort food collection • Korean-inspired boneless beef braise 2", "Slow cooker alternative", "Put beef, onion, sauce ingredients and only 100ml water in the slow cooker. Cook on LOW for", "approximately 7–9 hours (or HIGH for 4–6 hours), until the beef is nearly tender. Add carrots, mooli and", "shiitake for roughly the final 2–3 hours on LOW, allowing enough time for vegetables to soften. Transfer", "the cooking liquid to a saucepan, skim fat and reduce to a glossy sauce; pour back over the beef.", "Browning is optional. Exact timing varies with your cooker and beef cut.", "Five useful tips", "Keep the pear: It lends subtle sweetness and balances the soy, garlic and ginger.", "Skip beef stock: The beef, soy, mushrooms and aromatics produce plenty of savoury depth.", "Try dried shiitake: Use a little strained soaking liquid in place of water for extra umami.", "Reduce carefully: Soy becomes saltier as the liquid evaporates. Stop early or dilute if needed.", "Add contrast: Kimchi or sharp pickles cut beautifully through the sweet, rich sauce.", "Optional heat: Add 1 tsp gochugaru for mild warmth, or 1 tbsp gochujang for a spicier, less traditional sauce.", "Cut choice: Chuck gives tender chunks that hold their shape (ideal first attempt); shin gives a richer, gelatinous", "result and may need a longer braise. Refrigerate leftovers promptly and reheat until piping hot."], "pdf": "Korean_Slow_Braised_Boneless_Beef_Galbi_Jjim.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["800g boneless beef chuck / braising steak (preferred), or beef shin, cut into 4–5cm chunks", "2 medium carrots, cut into large pieces", "250g mooli (daikon radish), cut into large chunks", "150g shiitake mushrooms, fresh or rehydrated dried", "1 medium onion, quartered", "3 spring onions, sliced, for serving", "100ml regular soy sauce (not dark soy sauce)", "1 ripe pear, grated (a Conference pear is fine)", "5 garlic cloves, finely grated or minced", "20g fresh ginger, grated", "2 tbsp mirin", "1 tbsp brown sugar", "1 tbsp toasted sesame oil", "300ml water for oven method; only 100ml for slow cooker", "½ tsp freshly ground black pepper", "Steamed short-grain rice, toasted sesame seeds and kimchi or another sharp pickle"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "20–25 min", "cook": "3–4 hr oven / 7–9 hr slow cooker", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "swedish-saffron-fish-soup", "title": "Swedish saffron fish soup", "category": "Soups", "description": "Salmon, white fish and fennel in a golden broth with aioli.", "lines": ["Creamy Swedish Saffron Fish Soup", "Salmon, cod, fennel, dill and a golden saffron broth, finished with aioli. Adapted from Tareq Taylor’s", "gräddiga fisksoppa.", "Serves: 4 generous portions | Prep: 15 minutes | Cook: 30 minutes | Difficulty: Easy", "Ingredients - soup", "• 300g skinless salmon fillet, cut into large cubes", "• 300g skinless cod or haddock fillet, cut into large cubes", "• 500g waxy potatoes (such as Charlotte), diced", "• 1 medium leek, sliced; 2 carrots, diced; 1 fennel bulb, thinly sliced", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely chopped; 25g butter; 1 tbsp olive or rapeseed oil", "• 0.25–0.5g saffron (start with 0.25g for a gentler flavour)", "• 150ml dry white wine; 750ml fish stock; 200ml double cream", "• 1 small bunch fresh dill (stalks and leaves separated); 1 bay leaf", "• Finely grated zest of ½ lemon; 1–2 tsp lemon juice", "• Freshly ground black pepper; salt to taste", "To serve", "• 4 tbsp aioli (shop-bought is fine)", "• Crusty sourdough or baguette, plus extra fresh dill", "• Optional: 150g cooked peeled prawns, defrosted safely if frozen", "Method", "1. Bloom the saffron. Place the saffron in a small cup with 2 tbsp warm water. Leave for 5–10 minutes.", "2. Soften the vegetables. Melt butter with oil in a large saucepan. Add leek, carrots and fennel; cook gently", "for 8–10 minutes without browning.", "3. Build the flavour. Stir in garlic and finely chopped dill stalks. Cook for 1 minute. Add white wine and", "simmer for 2–3 minutes.", "4. Simmer the broth. Add potatoes, fish stock, saffron with its soaking liquid and bay leaf. Simmer, partially", "covered, for 12–15 minutes, until potatoes are tender.", "5. Add cream. Stir in double cream and lemon zest. Return to a gentle simmer. Add black pepper and taste", "before salting.", "6. Poach the fish. Lower the heat and add salmon and cod. Gently simmer for roughly 4–6 minutes, until", "opaque and cooked through; do not boil vigorously.", "7. Finish. Stir in chopped dill leaves and 1 tsp lemon juice. Taste and add more lemon as needed. If using", "cooked prawns, add at the end and heat until piping hot.", "8. Serve. Ladle into bowls, top with a spoonful of aioli and extra dill, and serve with crusty bread.", "Make-ahead and cooking notes", "• The vegetable-and-stock base can be cooked through step 4, cooled promptly and refrigerated for up to 24 hours.", "Reheat thoroughly before adding cream and fresh fish.", "• Freeze the base before adding fish and cream. Avoid slow-cooking the fish for hours: it only needs a few minutes at the", "end.", "• This version uses 600g fish and slightly less liquid than the inspiration recipe, to make a more substantial supper.", "• A little lemon balances the richness; keep the saffron and fennel prominent rather than adding smoked fish.", "2", "Inspired by: https://www.koket.se/tareqs-graddiga-fisksoppa"], "pdf": "Creamy_Swedish_Saffron_Fish_Soup.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["300g skinless salmon fillet, cut into large cubes", "300g skinless cod or haddock fillet, cut into large cubes", "500g waxy potatoes (such as Charlotte), diced", "1 medium leek, sliced", "2 carrots, diced", "1 fennel bulb, thinly sliced", "2 garlic cloves, finely chopped", "25g butter", "1 tbsp olive or rapeseed oil", "0.25–0.5g saffron (start with 0.25g for a gentler flavour)", "150ml dry white wine", "750ml fish stock", "200ml double cream", "1 small bunch fresh dill (stalks and leaves separated)", "1 bay leaf", "Finely grated zest of ½ lemon", "1–2 tsp lemon juice", "Freshly ground black pepper", "salt to taste", "4 tbsp aioli (shop-bought is fine)", "Crusty sourdough or baguette, plus extra fresh dill", "Optional: 150g cooked peeled prawns, defrosted safely if frozen"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "30 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [3]}}, {"id": "slow-cooked-beef-stroganoff", "title": "Slow-cooked beef stroganoff", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Tender beef, chestnut mushrooms and Dijon crème fraîche sauce.", "lines": ["Tender beef, chestnut mushrooms, Dijon mustard and a rich crème fraîche sauce. Adapted from", "Tareq Taylor’s Swedish Biff Stroganoff for slow, largely hands-off cooking.", "Serves: 4 generously Prep: 20–25 minutes Oven: 2½–3½ hours Difficulty: Easy", "Ingredients", "• 800g beef chuck/braising steak (or beef shin), cut into 3–4cm cubes", "• 2 medium onions, finely sliced; 250g chestnut mushrooms, thickly sliced", "• 3 garlic cloves, chopped; 30g butter; 1 tbsp rapeseed or vegetable oil", "• 2 tbsp tomato purée; 1 tbsp sweet paprika", "• 150ml dry white wine or dry sherry; 300ml low-salt beef stock", "• 1 tbsp Dijon mustard (plus extra to taste); 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce; 1 bay leaf", "• 150ml full-fat crème fraîche; 50ml double cream (optional)", "• 1 tsp red wine vinegar or lemon juice; salt and black pepper", "• To serve: buttery mashed potatoes, egg noodles or rice; fresh parsley; cornichons or pickled gherkins", "Oven method - my preferred version", "1. Brown the beef (8–10 min)", "Heat the oil in a heavy, ovenproof casserole over medium-high heat. Season beef lightly and brown in", "2–3 batches, developing a good crust. Transfer to a plate.", "2. Soften onions and mushrooms (about 12 min)", "Lower heat to medium. Add butter, onion and mushrooms. Cook until onions soften and mushroom", "moisture largely evaporates; allow a little browning. Add garlic and cook 1 minute.", "3. Build the sauce (5 min)", "Stir in tomato purée; cook 2 minutes. Reduce heat, stir in paprika for 30 seconds, then add wine or", "sherry and scrape up the browned bits. Add stock, mustard, Worcestershire sauce and bay leaf. Return", "beef and juices.", "4. Slow braise (2½–3½ hours)", "Bring to a gentle simmer. Cover and place in a preheated oven at 150°C conventional / 130°C fan. Cook", "until fork-tender, checking once partway through and adding a splash of water if drying. Beef shin may", "need longer.", "5. Finish the sauce (5 min)", "Remove bay leaf. If sauce is watery, briefly reduce it before adding dairy. Let bubbling settle, then stir", "in crème fraîche and optional cream. Warm gently; avoid a vigorous boil.", "6. Serve", "Add vinegar or lemon juice. Taste and adjust mustard, salt and pepper. Finish with parsley; serve over", "mash, noodles or rice, with gherkins on the side.", "Other ways to cook it", "Slow cooker - LOW 7–9 hours / HIGH 4–5 hours", "Brown beef and cook the onions/mushrooms as above; deglaze with the wine and transfer everything to", "the slow cooker. Reduce beef stock to 150ml because less liquid evaporates. Cook until fork-tender. If", "thin, thicken with 1–2 tsp cornflour mixed with cold water. Add crème fraîche and cream for the final", "15–20 minutes and heat through thoroughly.", "Hob - about 2½–3½ hours", "Follow the oven preparation, then cover and cook over the lowest heat that maintains a very gentle", "simmer. Stir and check the liquid periodically to prevent catching. Add dairy only after beef is tender.", "Small details that make it excellent", "• Brown the chestnut mushrooms before slow cooking for an earthier flavour.", "• Dry sherry adds a nutty savoury note; use white wine if preferred.", "• Add Dijon and a little vinegar at the end to balance the richness.", "• Full-fat crème fraîche is less prone to splitting than low-fat alternatives; never boil it hard.", "• Chuck retains soft, distinct pieces of beef; shin gives a silkier, more gelatinous sauce.", "• For freezing, ideally freeze the braised beef and sauce before adding dairy. Cool promptly, refrigerate", "leftovers and reheat thoroughly.", "Adapted from: https://www.koket.se/tareqs-biff-stroganoff"], "pdf": "Slow_Cooked_Beef_Stroganoff.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["800g beef chuck/braising steak (or beef shin), cut into 3–4cm cubes", "2 medium onions, finely sliced", "250g chestnut mushrooms, thickly sliced", "3 garlic cloves, chopped", "30g butter", "1 tbsp rapeseed or vegetable oil", "2 tbsp tomato purée", "1 tbsp sweet paprika", "150ml dry white wine or dry sherry", "300ml low-salt beef stock", "1 tbsp Dijon mustard (plus extra to taste)", "1 tsp Worcestershire sauce", "1 bay leaf", "150ml full-fat crème fraîche", "50ml double cream (optional)", "1 tsp red wine vinegar or lemon juice", "salt and black pepper", "To serve: buttery mashed potatoes, egg noodles or rice; fresh parsley; cornichons or pickled gherkins"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "20–25 min", "cook": "2½–3½ hr oven", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [2]}}, {"id": "winter-goulash-soup-garlic-bread", "title": "Winter goulash soup & garlic bread", "category": "Soups", "description": "Paprika-scented beef and root vegetables with garlic sourdough.", "lines": ["Serves: 4–6 Time: 2½–3 hours (mostly unattended) Equipment: heavy casserole or large pot", "Ingredients · Goulash", "• 800g beef chuck / braising steak, cut into 3cm cubes", "• 2 medium onions, finely chopped; 3 garlic cloves, crushed", "• 2 red peppers, bite-sized pieces; 2 medium carrots, thickly sliced", "• 1 parsnip, diced; 500g potatoes, peeled and cubed", "• 2 tbsp tomato purée; 2 tbsp sweet paprika (ideally Hungarian)", "• 1 tsp smoked paprika; 1 tsp caraway seeds, lightly crushed", "• ¼ tsp cayenne pepper (optional); 2 bay leaves", "• 330ml amber lager or malty beer; 600ml low-salt beef stock", "• 1 tbsp Worcestershire sauce; 1 tbsp red wine vinegar", "• 1 tbsp butter and 1 tbsp neutral cooking oil; salt and black pepper", "Ingredients · Garlic bread & serving", "• 4–6 thick slices sourdough bread", "• 50g softened butter; 2 garlic cloves, finely grated", "• Small handful fresh parsley, chopped", "• 150ml soured cream, to serve", "Method", "1. Brown the beef · ~10 min", "Heat the oil and butter in a heavy casserole over medium-high heat. Brown the beef in 2–3 batches,", "seasoning lightly. Transfer to a plate. Leave the browned bits in the pan.", "2. Soften the onions · 8–10 min", "Lower the heat to medium-low. Cook the onions until soft and lightly golden. Add garlic for 1 minute,", "then the tomato purée for 2 minutes.", "3. Bloom the spices · 30 seconds", "Turn heat to low (or take the pan off the heat). Stir in both paprikas, caraway and cayenne for around 30", "seconds, then immediately add a splash of beer. Avoid scorching the paprika.", "4. Gently braise · 1½–2 hours", "Return beef and resting juices. Add remaining beer, stock, Worcestershire sauce, bay leaves, carrots and", "parsnip. Bring to a gentle simmer; cover with the lid slightly ajar. Simmer around 90 minutes, then", "check. Continue until beef is nearly fork-tender, often another 30–45 minutes. Add water if necessary.", "5. Add potatoes & peppers · 20–25 min", "Stir in potato cubes and red peppers. Simmer until the potatoes are tender and the beef is completely", "soft. For a thicker soup, mash a few cooked potato pieces against the side of the pot.", "6. Bake garlic bread · 8–10 min", "Preheat oven to 200°C / 180°C fan. Mix softened butter, grated garlic, chopped parsley and a pinch of", "salt. Spread over bread; bake until crisp and golden at the edges.", "7. Finish & serve", "Remove bay leaves; stir in red wine vinegar. Taste and adjust salt, pepper and acidity. Serve with soured", "cream and hot garlic bread.", "Small details that make it fabulous", "• Use fresh, good-quality sweet paprika; it should remain the dominant flavour.", "• Choose malty beer rather than a strongly hopped IPA, which can become bitter when cooked.", "• Add peppers late so they stay recognisable rather than dissolving into the broth.", "• Keep it a hearty soup rather than a thick stew; add a splash of stock if needed.", "• For make-ahead cooking, braise the beef and vegetables first, then add potatoes when reheating; cool", "and refrigerate promptly, and reheat thoroughly.", "Note: Smoked paprika and Worcestershire sauce are flavour-led additions rather than traditional Hungarian goulash", "ingredients.", "Adapted from the Swedish Köket recipe “Klassisk gulaschsoppa med vitlöksbröd”:", "https://www.koket.se/klassisk-gulaschsoppa-med-vitloksbrod"], "pdf": "Ultimate_Winter_Goulash_Soup_with_Garlic_Bread.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["800g beef chuck / braising steak, cut into 3cm cubes", "2 medium onions, finely chopped", "3 garlic cloves, crushed", "2 red peppers, bite-sized pieces", "2 medium carrots, thickly sliced", "1 parsnip, diced", "500g potatoes, peeled and cubed", "2 tbsp tomato purée", "2 tbsp sweet paprika (ideally Hungarian)", "1 tsp smoked paprika", "1 tsp caraway seeds, lightly crushed", "¼ tsp cayenne pepper (optional)", "2 bay leaves", "330ml amber lager or malty beer", "600ml low-salt beef stock", "1 tbsp Worcestershire sauce", "1 tbsp red wine vinegar", "1 tbsp butter and 1 tbsp neutral cooking oil", "salt and black pepper", "4–6 thick slices sourdough bread", "50g softened butter", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated", "Small handful fresh parsley, chopped", "150ml soured cream, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4–6", "prep": "25–30 min*", "cook": "2–2½ hr*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "sk-nsk-kalops-with-beef-shin", "title": "Skånsk kalops with beef shin", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Southern Swedish beef stew with allspice, bay and pickled beetroot.", "lines": ["About 3 hours | 4 servings | Easy, mostly unattended", "Ingredients", "• 700g beef shin, cut into 3–4cm cubes", "• 2 medium onions, roughly chopped", "• 3 carrots, cut into thick rounds", "• 30g butter", "• 1 tbsp plain flour", "• 500ml beef stock, plus extra if necessary", "• 8–10 whole allspice berries, lightly crushed", "• 2 bay leaves", "• Salt and freshly ground black pepper", "• Boiled potatoes and pickled beetroot, to serve", "Method", "1. Pat the beef dry and season lightly. Melt the butter in a heavy casserole over medium-high heat; brown", "the shin in batches, then transfer to a plate.", "2. Lower the heat, add the onions and cook for 5–7 minutes until softened. Return the beef and its juices", "to the pan.", "3. Sprinkle over the flour and stir for a minute. Gradually add the stock, scraping up the browned bits from", "the base of the pan.", "4. Add the crushed allspice and bay leaves. Bring just to a simmer, cover and cook very gently for about", "1½ hours. Stir occasionally and add a splash of water if needed.", "5. Add the carrots and continue cooking, covered, for another 45–75 minutes, until the shin is completely", "tender. Beef shin may need longer; judge by tenderness, not the clock.", "6. Remove the bay leaves and any large spice pieces. Adjust the seasoning. If the gravy is too thin,", "simmer uncovered briefly; if too thick, add a splash of stock or water.", "Make it especially good", "• Beef shin is ideal: its connective tissue softens during slow cooking and enriches the gravy.", "• Keep the heat low for a gentle simmer; hard boiling can toughen the meat and reduce the liquid too quickly.", "• Oven alternative: after the stew first simmers, cover and cook at about 150°C conventional / 130°C fan; check", "the liquid and tenderness periodically.", "• Kalops often tastes even better the following day. Cool promptly and refrigerate, then reheat until piping hot.", "To serve: Buttery boiled potatoes and pickled beetroot. The acidity balances the rich gravy."], "pdf": "Skansk_Kalops_Beef_Shin.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["700g beef shin, cut into 3–4cm cubes", "2 medium onions, roughly chopped", "3 carrots, cut into thick rounds", "30g butter", "1 tbsp plain flour", "500ml beef stock, plus extra if necessary", "8–10 whole allspice berries, lightly crushed", "2 bay leaves", "Salt and freshly ground black pepper", "Boiled potatoes and pickled beetroot, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "20 min*", "cook": "2½–3 hr*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "smoked-haddock-plokkfiskur", "title": "Smoked haddock plokkfiskur", "category": "Fish & seafood", "description": "A thick, buttery Icelandic-style fish and potato dish.", "lines": ["35–40 minutes | 2 generous servings | Easy", "Ingredients", "• 350–400g smoked haddock, ideally undyed", "• 400g floury potatoes (Maris Piper or King Edward)", "• 1 medium onion, finely chopped", "• 35g butter", "• 30g plain flour", "• 300ml whole milk, plus a splash if needed", "• 1 bay leaf", "• Freshly ground black pepper", "• Chopped chives or parsley (optional)", "Method", "1. Peel the potatoes, cut into 2–3cm pieces and simmer in unsalted water for 12–15 minutes until tender.", "Drain well.", "2. Put the smoked haddock in a shallow pan with the milk and bay leaf. Heat until barely simmering, then", "gently poach for 5–8 minutes, until opaque and flaky.", "3. Lift out the haddock, reserving the milk. Remove any skin and bones and flake the fish into generous", "pieces. Discard the bay leaf.", "4. Melt the butter in a saucepan and gently cook the onion for about 8 minutes, until soft and sweet but", "not browned.", "5. Stir in the flour and cook for 1–2 minutes. Slowly whisk in the reserved smoky milk; simmer 3–5 minutes", "until thick and creamy.", "6. Fold in the drained potatoes, breaking up a few pieces to thicken the sauce. Gently fold in the smoked", "fish. Add black pepper and taste before adding any salt. Loosen with extra milk only if needed.", "Make it especially good", "• Reserve the poaching milk: it carries the smoky fish flavour into the sauce.", "• Do not mash everything smooth; the best texture has identifiable flakes of fish and chunks of potato.", "• For a gentler smoke flavour, replace half the smoked haddock with unsmoked haddock.", "To serve: Dark rye bread and butter, with chives or parsley if you like."], "pdf": "Smoked_Haddock_Plokkfiskur.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["350–400g smoked haddock, ideally undyed", "400g floury potatoes (Maris Piper or King Edward)", "1 medium onion, finely chopped", "35g butter", "30g plain flour", "300ml whole milk, plus a splash if needed", "1 bay leaf", "Freshly ground black pepper", "Chopped chives or parsley (optional)"], "facts": {"serves": "2", "prep": "10 min*", "cook": "25–30 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "finnish-salmon-soup-lohikeitto", "title": "Finnish salmon soup (lohikeitto)", "category": "Soups", "description": "Salmon, buttery leeks, potatoes and fresh dill.", "lines": ["35 minutes | 3 generous servings | Easy", "Ingredients", "• 350–400g skinless salmon fillet, cut into 3cm cubes", "• 450g waxy potatoes (e.g. Charlotte), peeled and diced", "• 1 large leek, washed and sliced", "• 2 medium carrots, sliced", "• 30g butter", "• 750ml fish or vegetable stock", "• 150ml double cream", "• 1 bay leaf", "• 5 whole allspice berries (optional)", "• A generous handful of fresh dill, chopped", "• Black pepper and a little lemon juice", "Method", "1. Melt the butter in a large saucepan. Add the leek and cook gently for 5–7 minutes, until soft but not", "browned.", "2. Add the potatoes, carrots, stock, bay leaf and allspice berries, if using. Bring to a gentle simmer.", "3. Simmer for 12–15 minutes, until the potatoes and carrots are just tender.", "4. Reduce the heat. Add the salmon and double cream and simmer very gently for 4–6 minutes, until the", "fish is opaque and cooked through. Avoid a vigorous boil.", "5. Remove the bay leaf and allspice berries. Add chopped dill, black pepper and a small squeeze of lemon.", "Taste and adjust seasoning.", "6. Rest off the heat for 5 minutes before ladling into bowls.", "Make it especially good", "• Use fresh dill generously: it is a defining flavour, not just a garnish.", "• Keep the salmon cubes large and avoid stirring too often, so they remain juicy and intact.", "• The finished soup should have a creamy broth rather than a thick sauce.", "To serve: Buttered rye bread or crusty sourdough."], "pdf": "Finnish_Salmon_Soup_Lohikeitto.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["350–400g skinless salmon fillet, cut into 3cm cubes", "450g waxy potatoes (e.g. Charlotte), peeled and diced", "1 large leek, washed and sliced", "2 medium carrots, sliced", "30g butter", "750ml fish or vegetable stock", "150ml double cream", "1 bay leaf", "5 whole allspice berries (optional)", "A generous handful of fresh dill, chopped", "Black pepper and a little lemon juice"], "facts": {"serves": "3", "prep": "10 min*", "cook": "25 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "japanese-curry-udon", "title": "Japanese curry udon", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "Savoury curry broth with pak choi and crispy chicken-style fillets.", "lines": ["Fillets", "Serves: 2 generously Time: 25–30 minutes Difficulty: Easy", "A richer, more savoury version of BBC Food’s speedy curry udon, retaining the crispy vegetarian chicken-style", "fillets and pak choi.", "Ingredients", "• 2 breaded vegetarian chicken-style fillets", "• 400g fresh udon noodles", "• 1 medium onion, thinly sliced", "• 2 small carrots, thinly sliced", "• 150g chestnut or shiitake mushrooms, sliced", "• 1 pak choi, quartered lengthways", "• 2 garlic cloves, grated", "• 2 tsp freshly grated ginger", "• 450–500ml chicken stock (start with 450ml)", "• About 46g S&B Golden Curry Medium Hot roux (half a 92g box); adjust to taste and packet guidance", "• 1 tbsp light soy sauce (start with less if stock is salty)", "• 1 tsp mirin, then adjust to taste", "• 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce (check vegetarian fillet suitability if relevant)", "• 10g butter", "• 1 tbsp neutral oil, plus a little for pak choi", "• Spring onions and toasted sesame seeds, to serve", "Method", "1. Cook the fillets. Cook the breaded chicken-style fillets in the oven or air fryer according to the packet. Time them to finish", "just before serving.", "2. Brown the mushrooms. Heat a little oil in a large deep frying pan. Fry mushrooms over medium-high heat until deeply", "golden; remove and set aside.", "3. Build the base. Add onion and carrots to the same pan. Cook 7–10 minutes over medium heat until the onion begins to", "colour. Add garlic and ginger for the last minute.", "4. Make the curry. Add 450ml chicken stock and the mushrooms. Simmer about 5 minutes until carrots are nearly tender.", "Lower heat, break in the curry roux and stir until fully dissolved.", "5. Season and adjust. Add soy sauce, mirin and Worcestershire sauce. Simmer gently until glossy. Add stock if too thick;", "simmer uncovered if too thin. Taste before adding salt.", "6. Sear the pak choi. In a separate hot pan, sear cut-side down for 2–3 minutes in a little oil. Add a splash of water, cover and", "steam another 1–2 minutes. A few drops of sesame oil are optional.", "7. Finish the noodles. Prepare udon according to packet instructions; add to the curry just long enough to heat through. Stir in", "butter off the heat.", "8. Serve. Divide noodles and sauce between bowls. Add pak choi and sliced crispy fillets. Finish with spring onions and", "toasted sesame seeds.", "Tips that make the difference", "• Keep the fillets crisp: Never simmer them in the sauce; slice and place on top just before eating.", "2", "• Do not overcook fresh udon: It is often precooked and needs only brief heating.", "• Control thickness: Japanese curry roux thickens as it stands. Start with less liquid, but keep extra stock handy.", "• Mind the salt: Roux, chicken stock, soy and Worcestershire sauce all contribute seasoning.", "• Balance sweetness: Golden Curry already has sweetness, so start with just 1 tsp mirin.", "• Optional toppings: A 6½–7 minute jammy egg, Japanese fukujinzuke pickles or ½ tsp chilli crisp per bowl.", "Shopping note", "S&B Golden Curry Medium Hot (92g) is a practical UK supermarket choice. Look at Tesco, Sainsbury’s or Ocado, subject to", "local stock. Use approximately half a box for two portions and adjust sauce thickness and intensity to preference.", "Original inspiration: https://www.bbc.co.uk/food/recipes/speedy_curry_udon_37900"], "pdf": "Japanese_Curry_Udon_Improved_Recipe.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["2 breaded vegetarian chicken-style fillets", "400g fresh udon noodles", "1 medium onion, thinly sliced", "2 small carrots, thinly sliced", "150g chestnut or shiitake mushrooms, sliced", "1 pak choi, quartered lengthways", "2 garlic cloves, grated", "2 tsp freshly grated ginger", "450–500ml chicken stock (start with 450ml)", "About 46g S&B Golden Curry Medium Hot roux (half a 92g box); adjust to taste and packet guidance", "1 tbsp light soy sauce (start with less if stock is salty)", "1 tsp mirin, then adjust to taste", "1 tsp Worcestershire sauce (check vegetarian fillet suitability if relevant)", "10g butter", "1 tbsp neutral oil, plus a little for pak choi", "Spring onions and toasted sesame seeds, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "2", "prep": "10 min*", "cook": "15–20 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [1]}}, {"id": "tofu-reuben-toastie", "title": "Tofu Reuben toastie", "category": "Vegetable dishes", "description": "Smoky tofu, Swiss cheese, sauerkraut and tangy dressing on rye.", "lines": ["MAKES 2 generous toasties PREP 15 min + marinating COOK 18–30 min", "Ingredients", "TOFU & MARINADE", "• 300g extra-firm tofu, sliced 3–5mm thick", "• 1 tbsp reduced-salt light soy sauce", "• 2 tbsp pickled beetroot juice", "• 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce", "• 1 tsp smoked paprika", "• ¼ tsp garlic granules", "• ¼ tsp onion granules", "• ¼ tsp ground black pepper", "• 1 tsp water, if needed to loosen marinade", "• Optional: ⅛ tsp Marmite for extra umami", "REUBEN DRESSING", "• 3 tbsp mayonnaise", "• 2 tsp Dijon mustard", "• 1 tbsp tomato ketchup", "• 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce", "• 1 tsp finely chopped gherkin", "• Pinch of smoked paprika", "• Optional: few drops lemon juice or ½ tsp prepared", "horseradish", "FOR THE SANDWICHES", "• 4 slices rye sourdough (ordinary sourdough is fine)", "• 70g Emmental, grated", "• 50g Gruyère, grated", "• 120g sauerkraut, well drained", "• 60–80g gherkins, thinly sliced", "• 20–30g softened butter, for the bread", "BEFORE YOU START Dry the tofu with kitchen paper. Squeeze moisture from the sauerkraut, and pat the", "gherkins dry. These small steps are the difference between crisp toast and soggy bread.", "Adapted from Rhian Melvin’s Tofu Reuben toastie · BBC Food 2 / 2", "METHOD / THE DETAILS MATTER", "Make the toasties", "Build crispness and flavour in stages. The tofu can be cooked ahead; fry the assembled sandwiches just", "before eating.", "01 Marinate the tofu. Stir the marinade ingredients together in a shallow dish. Add the dried tofu slices", "and turn gently to coat. Leave for 20–30 minutes (or cover and refrigerate overnight).", "02 Cook until chewy at the centre. Air-fry the slices in one layer at 180°C for 10–12 minutes, turning", "halfway, until coloured with crisp edges but not brittle. Alternatively, bake at 200°C / 180°C fan for", "20–25 minutes.", "03 Mix the dressing. Stir together the mayonnaise, Dijon, ketchup, Worcestershire sauce, minced", "gherkin and paprika. Taste before adding lemon or horseradish; the sauerkraut already brings plenty", "of acidity.", "04 Prepare the moist fillings. Squeeze the sauerkraut in a sieve, then warm it briefly in a dry frying pan", "to drive off excess liquid if needed. Pat the sliced gherkins dry. Mix the two cheeses together.", "05 Layer with care. Spread a thin layer of dressing on the inner faces of the bread. Add a little grated", "cheese against each slice, then pile on the tofu, sauerkraut and gherkins. Finish with the remaining", "cheese and close the sandwiches.", "06 Toast slowly. Butter the outside of both sandwiches. Fry in a large pan over medium-low heat for", "3–4 minutes per side, pressing gently with a spatula, until golden and the cheese has melted. Cover", "briefly near the end if necessary; there is usually no need to add water.", "07 Rest and serve. Wait about 1 minute before slicing, so the filling settles. Serve straightaway, with", "extra pickles on the side if you like.", "Why these tweaks work", "Best cheese: Emmental melts smoothly; Gruyère", "adds savoury depth. Grating them gives an even", "melt.", "Best bread: Rye sourdough brings a malty, slightly", "tangy edge. Keep the heat low enough to toast the", "crust without scorching it.", "Best texture: Thin tofu develops browned edges but", "stays tender. Thoroughly draining the sauerkraut", "stops the toastie becoming wet.", "Salt: The BBC original lists 7.31g per serving. This", "adaptation reduces the soy sauce and leaves", "Marmite optional, but still contains several salty", "ingredients.", "Inspired by Rhian Melvin’s original vegan recipe on BBC Food. This version uses dairy cheese, ordinary mayonnaise and", "Worcestershire sauce, so it is not vegan. Nutrition will differ from the original."], "pdf": "Improved_Tofu_Reuben_Toastie.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["300g extra-firm tofu, sliced 3–5mm thick", "1 tbsp reduced-salt light soy sauce", "2 tbsp pickled beetroot juice", "1 tsp Worcestershire sauce", "1 tsp smoked paprika", "¼ tsp garlic granules", "¼ tsp onion granules", "¼ tsp ground black pepper", "1 tsp water, if needed to loosen marinade", "Optional: ⅛ tsp Marmite for extra umami", "3 tbsp mayonnaise", "2 tsp Dijon mustard", "1 tbsp tomato ketchup", "1 tsp Worcestershire sauce", "1 tsp finely chopped gherkin", "Pinch of smoked paprika", "Optional: few drops lemon juice or ½ tsp prepared", "4 slices rye sourdough (ordinary sourdough is fine)", "70g Emmental, grated", "50g Gruyère, grated", "120g sauerkraut, well drained", "60–80g gherkins, thinly sliced", "20–30g softened butter, for the bread"], "facts": {"serves": "2 toasties", "prep": "15 min + marinating", "cook": "18–30 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "sticky-honey-chilli-peanut-chicken", "title": "Sticky honey, chilli & peanut chicken", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Golden chicken thighs with a spicy peanut glaze and fresh lime.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 TIME 40–45 minutes OVEN 200°C fan", "Ingredients", "Chicken", "• 1kg boneless, skinless chicken thighs, cut", "into 3–4cm pieces", "• 1 tbsp groundnut or vegetable oil", "• 1 tbsp light soy sauce", "Vegetables & finishing", "• 400g broccoli florets", "• 200g green beans, trimmed", "• 1 cucumber", "• 60g roasted peanuts, roughly chopped", "• 2 spring onions, sliced", "• Remaining ½ lime, to serve", "• A little oil for roasting the vegetables", "Sticky peanut glaze", "• 120g smooth peanut butter", "• 3 tbsp light soy sauce", "• 1 tbsp oyster sauce", "• 2 tbsp runny honey", "• 1–2 tbsp chilli paste, to taste", "• 1 tbsp freshly grated ginger", "• 3 garlic cloves, crushed", "• 1 tbsp rice vinegar", "• Juice of ½ lime", "• 1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "• 2–4 tbsp hot water, as needed", "To serve: Excellent with jasmine rice. For a noodle version, toss cooked udon in any extra glaze", "loosened with a little hot water.", "Method", "For sticky, caramelised chicken, roast first and glaze towards the end.", "01 Preheat and season. Heat the oven to 200°C fan. Toss the chicken with 1 tbsp oil and 1", "tbsp light soy sauce. Spread in a single layer on a large metal baking tray; use two trays if", "needed so the pieces have room to brown.", "02 Roast the chicken. Roast for 15 minutes, until beginning to colour. If the tray has a lot of", "liquid, carefully pour off the excess before glazing.", "03 Mix the glaze. Meanwhile, whisk together the peanut butter, remaining soy sauce, oyster", "sauce, honey, chilli paste, ginger, garlic, vinegar, lime juice and sesame oil. Add just enough", "hot water (start with 2 tbsp) to make a thick, spreadable glaze.", "04 Roast the vegetables. Toss the broccoli and green beans with a little oil on a separate tray.", "Roast for roughly 12–18 minutes until tender and browned at the edges. Turn once if needed.", "05 Glaze and caramelise. Coat the partly roasted chicken thoroughly with the peanut mixture.", "Return to the oven for around 7–10 minutes, turning or checking as needed, until glossy and", "sticky. The thickest pieces must reach 75°C internally; cook longer if necessary. If the glaze", "darkens too quickly, loosely cover with foil.", "06 Finish and serve. Top the chicken with chopped roasted peanuts and spring onions. Serve", "alongside the roasted vegetables and sliced raw cucumber. Finish with a squeeze of fresh", "lime, plus rice or udon if you like.", "Make it even better", "Space out the chicken. Crowded trays trap steam and prevent browning; two trays are better than", "one crowded tray.", "Keep the glaze thick. Too much water turns it into a sauce rather than a sticky coating. Peanut", "butter brands vary, so add water gradually.", "Watch the final roast. Honey and peanuts can scorch. Aim for glossy, caramelised patches, not a", "dark burnt crust.", "Keep cucumber fresh. A little rice vinegar and lime on the sliced cucumber brings a bright, crunchy", "contrast to the rich chicken."], "pdf": "Sticky_Honey_Chilli_Peanut_Chicken.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["1kg boneless, skinless chicken thighs, cut into 3–4cm pieces", "1 tbsp groundnut or vegetable oil", "1 tbsp light soy sauce", "400g broccoli florets", "200g green beans, trimmed", "1 cucumber", "60g roasted peanuts, roughly chopped", "2 spring onions, sliced", "Remaining ½ lime, to serve", "A little oil for roasting the vegetables", "120g smooth peanut butter", "3 tbsp light soy sauce", "1 tbsp oyster sauce", "2 tbsp runny honey", "1–2 tbsp chilli paste, to taste", "1 tbsp freshly grated ginger", "3 garlic cloves, crushed", "1 tbsp rice vinegar", "Juice of ½ lime", "1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "2–4 tbsp hot water, as needed"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min*", "cook": "25–30 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "creamy-peanut-udon-with-sticky-tempeh", "title": "Creamy peanut udon with sticky tempeh", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "Silky peanut sauce, chilli-glazed tempeh and charred broccoli.", "lines": ["SERVES 2 generously PREP + COOK 35–40 minutes DIFFICULTY Easy", "Ingredients", "STICKY CHILLI TEMPEH", "• 200–250g tempeh, cut into 1–1.5cm cubes", "• 1 tbsp neutral cooking oil", "• 1 tbsp doubanjiang (chilli bean paste)", "• 1 tbsp regular oyster sauce", "• 1 garlic clove, crushed", "• 1 tsp freshly grated ginger", "• 1 tsp honey", "VEGETABLES & FINISHING", "• 200g Tenderstem broccoli", "• 2 spring onions, thinly sliced", "• 2 tbsp roasted peanuts, roughly chopped", "• Chilli crisp, to taste", "• Remaining ½ lime, cut into wedges", "• A little neutral oil for the broccoli", "CREAMY PEANUT UDON", "• 300g silken tofu", "• 4 tbsp smooth peanut butter (preferably", "unsweetened)", "• 1½ tbsp light soy sauce or tamari", "• 1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "• 1 garlic clove, crushed", "• 1 tsp freshly grated ginger", "• 2 tsp honey", "• Juice of ½ lime", "• 400g fresh udon noodles", "• A little reserved noodle cooking water", "Shopping note: Regular oyster sauce is used here, so the recipe is not vegan. The silken tofu and tempeh stay exactly where they", "belong.", "HOW TO MAKE IT", "Method", "1. Roast the tempeh", "Preheat the oven to 200°C fan. Toss the tempeh with 1 tbsp oil and spread in one layer on a baking tray.", "Roast for 20–25 minutes, turning halfway, until golden and crisp at the edges.", "2. Make it sticky", "Mix the doubanjiang, oyster sauce, garlic, ginger and honey. Toss the roasted tempeh in the glaze, then", "return it to the oven for 3–5 minutes until sticky and lightly caramelised. Watch closely so the glaze does", "not burn.", "3. Blend the peanut sauce", "Blend the silken tofu, peanut butter, soy sauce, sesame oil, garlic, ginger, honey and lime juice until", "completely smooth. Taste: it should be distinctly peanutty, savoury, gently sweet and bright. Add another", "spoonful of peanut butter if needed.", "4. Char the broccoli", "Heat a little oil in a frying pan over a medium-high heat. Cook the Tenderstem for about 5–8 minutes,", "turning, until browned at the edges. Add a splash of water and cover briefly if the thickest stalks need", "softening.", "5. Coat the udon", "Cook the fresh udon according to the packet (usually 1–2 minutes) until loosened. Reserve a mug of", "cooking water. Gently warm the peanut sauce in a large pan over low-to-medium heat with 3–4 tbsp of", "noodle water. Add udon and toss; loosen with more water a tablespoon at a time until glossy. Do not boil", "the sauce hard.", "6. Finish and serve", "Divide the creamy udon between two bowls. Top with sticky tempeh and charred broccoli. Add spring", "onions, chopped roasted peanuts, chilli crisp and a generous squeeze of fresh lime.", "Small details that make it better", "To tame tempeh bitterness: Steam the cubes for 8–10 minutes before roasting, then pat very dry. This is optional.", "For the best sauce: Keep the silken tofu, but taste after blending. Four tablespoons of peanut butter should give a", "clear roasted-peanut flavour; add more if you want it bolder.", "For balance: The glaze is salty and spicy, while the noodles are mild and creamy. Finish with lime and crunchy", "toppings. Taste before adding any extra salt."], "pdf": "Creamy_Peanut_Udon_with_Sticky_Tempeh.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["200–250g tempeh, cut into 1–1.5cm cubes", "1 tbsp neutral cooking oil", "1 tbsp doubanjiang (chilli bean paste)", "1 tbsp regular oyster sauce", "1 garlic clove, crushed", "1 tsp freshly grated ginger", "1 tsp honey", "200g Tenderstem broccoli", "2 spring onions, thinly sliced", "2 tbsp roasted peanuts, roughly chopped", "Chilli crisp, to taste", "Remaining ½ lime, cut into wedges", "A little neutral oil for the broccoli", "300g silken tofu", "4 tbsp smooth peanut butter (preferably unsweetened)", "1½ tbsp light soy sauce or tamari", "1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "1 garlic clove, crushed", "1 tsp freshly grated ginger", "2 tsp honey", "Juice of ½ lime", "400g fresh udon noodles", "A little reserved noodle cooking water"], "facts": {"serves": "2", "prep": "10–15 min*", "cook": "25–30 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "marry-me-chicken", "title": "Marry Me chicken", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Crisp-skinned chicken in a sun-dried tomato and Parmesan cream sauce.", "lines": ["Serves 4 · Prep 15 min · Cook 35-45 min · Best with new potatoes + greens", "Ingredients", "Chicken", "8 skin-on boneless chicken thighs (about 750-900g", "total)", "Salt and lots of black pepper", "1 tsp plain flour, optional, for the flesh side only", "Sauce", "1 tbsp oil from the sun-dried tomato jar, plus", "rendered chicken fat as needed", "1 red onion, finely sliced", "3 garlic cloves, finely grated or minced", "1 tsp dried oregano", "1/2 tsp chilli flakes", "60-75ml dry white wine", "125g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained and finely", "sliced", "250ml lower-salt chicken stock", "150ml double cream", "35-40g Parmesan, very finely grated", "1/2 lemon, finely zested", "1-2 tsp fresh lemon juice, plus more to taste", "15-20 basil leaves, torn", "To serve", "New potatoes or mash, plus green beans / broccoli", "Method", "1. Dry and season. Pat the chicken very dry. Season well with salt and", "black pepper. If using flour, dust only the flesh side very lightly - leave the", "skin bare so it can crisp.", "2. Render the skin. Put the thighs skin-side down in a cold or barely warm", "wide frying pan. Bring to medium heat and cook largely undisturbed for 7-10", "minutes, until a lot of fat has rendered and the skin is deep golden and crisp.", "Flip and cook the flesh side for 1-2 minutes, then remove to a plate.", "3. Control the fat. Pour off excess rendered fat, leaving about 1 tbsp in the", "pan. Add a little sun-dried tomato oil if needed.", "4. Build the base. Add the red onion with a pinch of salt and cook over", "medium-low heat for 8-10 minutes until very soft. Add the garlic, oregano", "and chilli flakes and cook for 45-60 seconds.", "5. Deglaze. Pour in the white wine. Scrape up every browned bit from the", "base of the pan and let the wine reduce by at least half.", "6. Make the sauce. Stir in the sun-dried tomatoes, stock and cream. Bring", "only to a gentle simmer.", "7. Finish the chicken gently. Return the thighs skin-side up, keeping the", "skin mostly above the sauce. Simmer uncovered for about 10-15 minutes, or", "until the thickest part reaches 75C. Remove the chicken once done so it", "cannot overcook.", "8. Finish the sauce off the heat. If the sauce is thin, simmer it for 1-2", "minutes first. Then lower the heat or take the pan off completely and stir in", "the Parmesan gradually. Add the lemon zest and 1 tsp lemon juice. Taste", "before adding more salt, lemon or pepper.", "9. Serve. Spoon the sauce onto plates and sit the chicken on top so the skin", "stays crisp. Scatter generously with torn basil and finish with black pepper.", "Serve extra lemon at the table.", "Why this version works: Skin-on thighs give more flavour and stay juicy; starting them in a cool pan renders the skin properly. White", "wine lifts the fond from the pan, lemon balances the cream and Parmesan, and the chicken finishes skin-side up so you keep the contrast", "between crisp skin and silky sauce.", "Optional extra-crisp finish: If the skin has softened at all, put the cooked thighs under a very hot grill for 1-2 minutes while you finish the sauce.", "Do not pour sauce over the skin at the table."], "pdf": "marry_me_chicken_best_version.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["8 skin-on boneless chicken thighs (about 750-900g total)", "Salt and lots of black pepper", "1 tsp plain flour, optional, for the flesh side only", "1 tbsp oil from the sun-dried tomato jar, plus rendered chicken fat as needed", "1 red onion, finely sliced", "3 garlic cloves, finely grated or minced", "1 tsp dried oregano", "1/2 tsp chilli flakes", "60-75ml dry white wine", "125g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained and finely sliced", "250ml lower-salt chicken stock", "150ml double cream", "35-40g Parmesan, very finely grated", "1/2 lemon, finely zested", "1-2 tsp fresh lemon juice, plus more to taste", "15-20 basil leaves, torn", "New potatoes or mash, plus green beans / broccoli"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "35–45 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "soy-chilli-lime-salmon-traybake", "title": "Soy, chilli & lime salmon traybake", "category": "Fish & seafood", "description": "A quick salmon traybake with a bright soy and lime dressing.", "lines": ["Serves: 2 Prep: 15 min Cook: 9-12 min", "INGREDIENTS", "Traybake", "• 2 x 180-200g salmon fillets, preferably skin-on", "• 100g Tenderstem broccoli", "• 100g green beans", "• 1 red pepper, sliced into strips", "• A little oil for the tray", "Quick marinade", "• Zest and juice of 1/2 lime", "• 1 tbsp soy sauce", "• 1/2 tsp caster sugar", "Fresh dressing", "• 1 small garlic clove, finely grated", "• 10-15g fresh ginger, finely grated", "• 2 spring onions, finely sliced", "• 1 red chilli, finely chopped", "• Zest and juice of remaining 1/2 lime", "• 1 tbsp soy sauce", "• 2 tsp extra-virgin olive oil", "• 1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "• 1/2 tsp caster sugar", "To finish", "• Small handful fresh coriander, roughly torn", "• 1-2 tsp sesame seeds, toasted", "• Lime wedges, optional", "• 120-140g dry jasmine rice, optional, to serve", "METHOD", "1. Heat the oven. Put a sturdy metal roasting tray in the oven and heat", "to 230C / 210C fan. A hot tray helps the vegetables roast rather than", "steam.", "2. Marinate the salmon. Mix the lime zest and juice, soy sauce and", "sugar. Coat the salmon and leave for 10-15 minutes. Do not leave it", "for hours.", "3. Make the dressing. Mix the garlic, ginger, spring onion, chilli, lime", "zest and juice, soy, olive oil, toasted sesame oil and sugar. Leave to", "stand while the salmon cooks.", "4. Prepare the greens. Cover the broccoli and beans with boiling", "water for 3-4 minutes, then drain and dry very thoroughly. Leave the", "pepper raw.", "5. Load the hot tray. Lightly oil it. Add broccoli, beans and pepper,", "then place the salmon skin-side down. The used marinade can go over", "the vegetables only if it goes straight into the hot oven and cooks fully.", "6. Roast. Cook for 9-12 minutes, checking from 9. The salmon should", "stay moist; the vegetables should be tender with a little bite and colour.", "7. Finish. Spoon the fresh dressing over the hot salmon and", "vegetables. Scatter with coriander and toasted sesame seeds. Serve", "immediately, with lime wedges and jasmine rice if using.", "BEST RESULTS", "Dry the vegetables. This is the easiest way to avoid a watery traybake.", "Use toasted sesame oil. One teaspoon gives the dressing a deeper", "roasted aroma.", "Keep the finishing dressing fresh. Do not cook it; the ginger, chilli, lime", "and coriander are the contrast to the rich salmon.", "Flavour profile: rich salmon, crisp-tender greens, sweet pepper, salty soy, sharp lime, chilli heat, fresh coriander and nutty sesame."], "pdf": "salmon_traybake_soy_chilli_lime_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["2 x 180-200g salmon fillets, preferably skin-on", "100g Tenderstem broccoli", "100g green beans", "1 red pepper, sliced into strips", "A little oil for the tray", "Zest and juice of 1/2 lime", "1 tbsp soy sauce", "1/2 tsp caster sugar", "1 small garlic clove, finely grated", "10-15g fresh ginger, finely grated", "2 spring onions, finely sliced", "1 red chilli, finely chopped", "Zest and juice of remaining 1/2 lime", "1 tbsp soy sauce", "2 tsp extra-virgin olive oil", "1 tsp toasted sesame oil", "1/2 tsp caster sugar", "Small handful fresh coriander, roughly torn", "1-2 tsp sesame seeds, toasted", "Lime wedges, optional", "120-140g dry jasmine rice, optional, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "2", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "9–12 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "greek-style-lemon-chicken-orzo-soup", "title": "Greek-style lemon chicken & orzo soup", "category": "Soups", "description": "Chicken, leek, spinach and dill with a fresh lemon finish.", "lines": ["Serves 4 · Prep 15 min · Cook 30-35 min · Best for Dinner / batch lunch", "INGREDIENTS", "• 500-600g chicken breast or boneless", "skinless thighs", "• 1 tbsp paprika", "• 1 tbsp garlic powder", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 2 carrots, diced", "• 2 small leeks, sliced and well washed", "• 6 garlic cloves, crushed or finely", "chopped", "• 2 tsp dried oregano", "• 1/2 tsp ground turmeric", "• 1 bay leaf", "• 1.5 litres good lower-salt chicken", "stock", "• 200g orzo", "• 60-100g baby spinach", "• finely grated zest of 1 lemon", "• juice of 1-2 lemons, to taste", "• 15-20g fresh dill, roughly chopped", "• freshly ground black pepper", "• salt, only as needed", "• pinch chilli flakes, optional", "• extra-virgin olive oil, to finish, optional", "WHY THIS VERSION", "WORKS", "The chicken is browned properly, the pan", "is deglazed, and the lemon and dill are", "added right at the end so the soup stays", "savoury but tastes fresh and distinctly", "lemony.", "METHOD", "1. Season the chicken. Pat the chicken dry. Toss with the paprika, garlic powder, a", "few grinds of black pepper and a small pinch of salt. If you have time, leave for", "10-15 minutes.", "2. Brown it well. Heat the olive oil in a large saucepan or casserole over", "medium-high heat. Brown the chicken until it has good golden patches, about 2-3", "minutes per side. It does not need to be cooked through. Transfer to a plate.", "3. Build the vegetable base. Lower the heat to medium. Add the carrots and leeks", "with a small pinch of salt and cook for 7-8 minutes, stirring occasionally, until", "softened and beginning to colour.", "4. Bloom the aromatics. Add the garlic, oregano and turmeric. Cook for 45-60", "seconds, stirring, until fragrant.", "5. Deglaze. Pour in about 100ml of the chicken stock and scrape the bottom of the", "pan thoroughly to dissolve the browned bits.", "6. Simmer the chicken. Add the remaining stock and bay leaf. Return the chicken", "to the pan. Bring just to a simmer, then cook gently until the chicken is cooked", "through, about 8-12 minutes depending on thickness.", "7. Shred. Lift the chicken onto a board and leave it for a few minutes. Shred or cut", "into generous bite-sized pieces. Discard the bay leaf.", "8. Cook the orzo. Bring the broth back to a gentle simmer. Add the orzo and cook", "until just al dente, usually 7-9 minutes. Stir frequently so it does not catch on the", "base.", "9. Finish gently. Return the chicken to the pan and add the spinach. Stir for 1-2", "minutes, just until the spinach wilts and the chicken is hot.", "10. Add the fresh flavours off the heat. Turn off the heat. Stir in the lemon zest,", "the juice of 1 lemon and most of the dill. Taste, then add more lemon, black pepper", "and salt only if needed.", "11. Serve. Ladle into bowls and finish with the remaining dill, optional chilli flakes", "and a small drizzle of extra-virgin olive oil.", "BATCH-COOKING NOTE", "If you are storing this for lunches, cook the orzo separately. Keep it chilled in its", "own container and add it to each portion when reheating. Otherwise the pasta keeps", "absorbing broth and the soup becomes much thicker overnight. Reheat gently, then", "refresh with a little extra lemon and dill.", "Serving idea: This soup is complete on its own, but warm crusty bread is excellent alongside. If using chicken breast, avoid", "over-simmering it so it stays tender."], "pdf": "greek_style_lemon_chicken_orzo_soup_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["500-600g chicken breast or boneless", "1 tbsp paprika", "1 tbsp garlic powder", "1 tbsp olive oil", "2 carrots, diced", "2 small leeks, sliced and well washed", "6 garlic cloves, crushed or finely", "2 tsp dried oregano", "1/2 tsp ground turmeric", "1 bay leaf", "1.5 litres good lower-salt chicken stock", "200g orzo", "60-100g baby spinach", "finely grated zest of 1 lemon", "juice of 1-2 lemons, to taste", "15-20g fresh dill, roughly chopped", "freshly ground black pepper", "salt, only as needed", "pinch chilli flakes, optional", "extra-virgin olive oil, to finish, optional"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "30–35 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "creamy-pesto-chickpea-pasta", "title": "Creamy pesto chickpea pasta", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "A creamy, herby weeknight pasta with chickpeas.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 TIME 20-25 mins STYLE Vegetarian", "INGREDIENTS", "• 280 g orecchiette or conchiglie", "• 1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained, rinsed and dried", "well", "• 1 tbsp olive oil, preferably from the sun-dried tomato", "jar", "• 70-80 g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained and sliced", "• 100 g baby spinach", "• 120 g full-fat creme fraiche", "• 90-100 g good-quality basil pesto", "• 1/2 lemon, finely grated zest plus 1-2 tsp juice", "• 30 g Parmesan or vegetarian hard cheese, finely", "grated, plus extra to serve", "• 20-25 g pine nuts, toasted (optional but excellent)", "• Small handful fresh basil leaves", "• Freshly ground black pepper", "• Salt, only if needed", "Why this version works", "Half the chickpeas stay soft; half are browned for nutty", "contrast. Pasta water gives a glossy sauce without needing", "lots of cream. Pesto and lemon go in off the heat so their", "fresh flavours stay vivid.", "METHOD", "1. Cook the pasta", "Bring a large pan of well-salted water to the boil. Cook the pasta", "until just al dente. Before draining, reserve 150 ml pasta water.", "2. Brown the chickpeas", "While the pasta cooks, heat the olive oil in a large frying pan", "over medium-high heat. Add half the chickpeas and leave", "them undisturbed for stretches so they colour. Fry for 5-7", "minutes until lightly crisp and golden. Add the remaining", "chickpeas for the final minute, just to warm through.", "3. Add the tomatoes and spinach", "Add the sun-dried tomatoes and toss for 30 seconds. Add the", "spinach and cook only until just wilted. Reduce the heat to low.", "4. Build the creamy sauce", "Add the drained pasta and creme fraiche. Toss well, adding", "reserved pasta water a splash at a time until the sauce becomes", "silky and clings to the pasta. Start with about 60 ml; use more", "as needed.", "5. Protect the pesto", "Take the pan off the heat. Stir through the pesto, lemon zest", "and 1 tsp lemon juice. Add most of the Parmesan and plenty of", "black pepper. Taste before adding any salt.", "6. Finish", "Adjust with a little more pasta water or lemon juice if needed.", "Divide between bowls and finish with the remaining Parmesan,", "torn basil and toasted pine nuts.", "BEST TIP Do not simmer the pesto. Stir it in off the heat: the basil stays fresher and the sauce tastes brighter.", "LEFTOVERS Reheat gently with a splash of water. Add a tiny spoonful of fresh pesto or squeeze of lemon after reheating to", "revive the flavour.", "Serving note: short, scoopy pasta such as orecchiette or conchiglie works especially well because it catches chickpeas, tomato pieces", "and sauce. If strict vegetarian cooking matters, choose a vegetarian hard cheese rather than traditional Parmesan."], "pdf": "creamy_pesto_chickpea_pasta_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["280 g orecchiette or conchiglie", "1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained, rinsed and dried well", "1 tbsp olive oil, preferably from the sun-dried tomato jar", "70-80 g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained and sliced", "100 g baby spinach", "120 g full-fat creme fraiche", "90-100 g good-quality basil pesto", "1/2 lemon, finely grated zest plus 1-2 tsp juice", "30 g Parmesan or vegetarian hard cheese, finely grated, plus extra to serve", "20-25 g pine nuts, toasted (optional but excellent)", "Small handful fresh basil leaves", "Freshly ground black pepper", "Salt, only if needed"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "5–10 min*", "cook": "15–20 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "wild-rice-mushroom-soup", "title": "Wild rice & mushroom soup", "category": "Soups", "description": "Deeply browned mushrooms, porcini, wild rice and crème fraîche.", "lines": ["Deeply browned mushrooms, wild rice, porcini, herbs and creme fraiche | Serves 4", "The key to this version: brown the mushrooms properly, use a little porcini soaking liquor for depth, and finish with", "lemon so the soup stays rich but not heavy.", "Ingredients", "• 1 heaped tbsp butter", "• 2 shallots, finely chopped (or 1 small", "onion)", "• 2 celery sticks, finely diced", "• 1 large carrot, finely diced", "• 5 garlic cloves, finely chopped", "• 2 tsp fresh thyme leaves", "• 1 tsp fresh rosemary, very finely chopped", "• 250g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "• 150g oyster mushrooms, torn or sliced", "• 10-15g dried porcini", "• 200ml boiling water, for soaking porcini", "• 50-75ml dry sherry or dry white wine", "(optional)", "• 2 tbsp dark soy sauce", "• 800ml low-salt vegetable stock", "• 400g cooked brown/wild rice mix", "(350-500g to taste)", "• 3 tbsp full-fat creme fraiche", "• 1-2 tsp fresh lemon juice, to taste", "• Freshly ground black pepper", "• Salt, only if needed", "• Small handful flat-leaf parsley, chopped", "• Parmesan or vegetarian hard cheese, to", "serve", "Method", "1. Soak the porcini. Cover with 200ml boiling water and leave for 15 minutes.", "Lift out, chop finely, and strain the soaking liquid through a very fine sieve or", "coffee filter. Keep the liquid.", "2. Build the vegetable base. Melt the butter in a large heavy saucepan over", "medium heat. Add the shallots, celery and carrot with a small pinch of salt.", "Cook for about 8 minutes, stirring occasionally, until softened and beginning to", "colour.", "3. Brown the mushrooms in batches. Push the vegetables to the side or", "remove them briefly. Increase the heat and cook the chestnut and oyster", "mushrooms in 2 batches so the pan is not crowded. Let them sit against the hot", "pan between stirs. Aim for deep golden-brown edges rather than pale, steamed", "mushrooms.", "4. Add the aromatics. Return everything to the pan. Add the garlic, thyme,", "rosemary and chopped porcini. Cook for about 1 minute, just until fragrant.", "5. Deglaze. Add the sherry or wine, if using, and scrape up the browned bits", "from the base of the pan. Let it bubble until almost evaporated. If skipping", "alcohol, use a splash of stock instead.", "6. Make the broth. Stir in the soy sauce, strained porcini liquid and 800ml", "vegetable stock. Bring to a gentle simmer and cook for 12-15 minutes so the", "flavours come together.", "7. Add the rice. Stir in about 400g cooked brown/wild rice and simmer for 3-5", "minutes. Add more rice if you want a thicker, more substantial soup.", "8. Finish gently. Lower the heat and stir in the creme fraiche. Do not boil", "vigorously after this point. Add 1 tsp lemon juice, plenty of black pepper, and", "taste. Add more lemon and salt only if needed.", "9. Serve. Ladle into bowls and finish with parsley and a modest grating of", "Parmesan or vegetarian hard cheese.", "Best-result notes", "• Do not overcrowd the mushrooms. Browning is the biggest flavour upgrade in this recipe.", "• Use low-salt stock: soy sauce, cheese and porcini all bring savoury intensity.", "• Lemon should brighten the soup, not make it taste lemony. Add it gradually at the end.", "• Leftovers thicken as the rice absorbs broth. Loosen with a splash of water or stock when reheating.", "Theme: Wild rice and mushroom soup | Nederlands: Wilde-rijstsoep met paddenstoelen | Deutsch: Wildreis-Pilzsuppe | Norsk bokmal:", "Villrissuppe med sopp"], "pdf": "wild_rice_mushroom_soup_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["1 heaped tbsp butter", "2 shallots, finely chopped (or 1 small onion)", "2 celery sticks, finely diced", "1 large carrot, finely diced", "5 garlic cloves, finely chopped", "2 tsp fresh thyme leaves", "1 tsp fresh rosemary, very finely chopped", "250g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "150g oyster mushrooms, torn or sliced", "10-15g dried porcini", "200ml boiling water, for soaking porcini", "50-75ml dry sherry or dry white wine (optional)", "2 tbsp dark soy sauce", "800ml low-salt vegetable stock", "400g cooked brown/wild rice mix (350-500g to taste)", "3 tbsp full-fat creme fraiche", "1-2 tsp fresh lemon juice, to taste", "Freshly ground black pepper", "Salt, only if needed", "Small handful flat-leaf parsley, chopped", "Parmesan or vegetarian hard cheese, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "20 min*", "cook": "40–45 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "root-vegetable-red-lentil-soup", "title": "Root vegetable & red lentil soup", "category": "Soups", "description": "A one-pot soup with toasted cumin, lemon and crunchy seeds.", "lines": ["Serves: 4 Prep: 15 min Cook: 35-40 min", "Ingredients", "• 400 g mixed root vegetables, peeled and diced -", "ideally 150 g carrot, 100 g parsnip, 100 g swede and", "50 g sweet potato or butternut squash", "• 150 g red lentils, rinsed thoroughly", "• 1 medium onion, finely chopped", "• 3 garlic cloves, finely chopped or grated", "• 1 1/2 tsp cumin seeds", "• 1/4-1/2 tsp crushed chilli flakes", "• 1 tbsp olive oil, plus a little extra if needed", "• 850-900 ml vegetable stock, plus extra hot stock or", "water to loosen", "• 1-2 tsp lemon juice, to taste", "• Black pepper and salt, to taste", "• Optional: 1/8-1/4 tsp MSG", "To finish", "• 2 tbsp mixed seeds - pumpkin, sunflower and/or", "sesame", "• Small pinch cumin and chilli for the seeds", "• Mature Cheddar or vegetarian hard cheese, finely", "grated", "• Extra-virgin olive oil and lemon, to finish", "Method", "1. Build the base. Heat the olive oil in a large saucepan. Add", "the onion with a pinch of salt and cook over medium heat for", "8-10 minutes until soft and just starting to colour.", "2. Brown the roots. Add the diced root vegetables and cook", "for 5-7 minutes, stirring occasionally, until a few surfaces are", "lightly golden.", "3. Bloom the spices. Push the vegetables aside. Add the", "cumin seeds and chilli to the exposed oil for about 30", "seconds. Add the garlic and cook for another 30-60 seconds,", "just until fragrant.", "4. Add lentils and stock. Stir in the rinsed lentils for 30", "seconds, then add 850-900 ml stock. Bring to a gentle", "simmer.", "5. Simmer. Cook partly covered for 20-25 minutes, stirring", "from time to time, until the roots and lentils are completely", "tender.", "6. Blend. Blend with a stick blender until creamy but not", "necessarily perfectly silky. Add hot stock or water a little at a", "time until the consistency suits you.", "7. Finish the flavour. Add 1 tsp lemon juice, plenty of black", "pepper, and salt as needed. Add a tiny pinch of MSG if using.", "Taste, then add more lemon if the soup still tastes flat or", "overly sweet.", "8. Toast the topping. Dry-toast the mixed seeds in a small", "frying pan with a pinch of cumin, chilli and salt until fragrant", "and lightly golden.", "9. Serve. Ladle into bowls and finish with toasted seeds, a", "little grated cheese, a small drizzle of extra-virgin olive oil", "and, if needed, one last squeeze of lemon.", "Key flavour tips", "Colour the vegetables rather than merely softening them; toast", "the cumin in the oil; and always add the lemon only at the end.", "Those three changes do most of the work.", "For leftovers", "The lentils will thicken the soup in the fridge. Reheat with a", "splash of water or stock, then re-check the salt and lemon", "before serving.", "Theme: Root vegetable and red lentil soup Nederlands: Wortelgroente- en rode-linzensoep Deutsch:", "Wurzelgemüse-Rote-Linsen-Suppe Norsk bokmål: Rotgrønnsaks- og rød linsesuppe"], "pdf": "root_vegetable_red_lentil_soup_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["400 g mixed root vegetables, peeled and diced - ideally 150 g carrot, 100 g parsnip, 100 g swede and 50 g sweet potato or butternut squash", "150 g red lentils, rinsed thoroughly", "1 medium onion, finely chopped", "3 garlic cloves, finely chopped or grated", "1 1/2 tsp cumin seeds", "1/4-1/2 tsp crushed chilli flakes", "1 tbsp olive oil, plus a little extra if needed", "850-900 ml vegetable stock, plus extra hot stock or", "1-2 tsp lemon juice, to taste", "Black pepper and salt, to taste", "Optional: 1/8-1/4 tsp MSG", "2 tbsp mixed seeds - pumpkin, sunflower and/or", "Small pinch cumin and chilli for the seeds", "Mature Cheddar or vegetarian hard cheese, finely", "Extra-virgin olive oil and lemon, to finish"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "35–40 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "chicken-pearl-barley-soup", "title": "Chicken & pearl barley soup", "category": "Soups", "description": "A deep broth with toasted spices, chunky chicken and fresh herbs.", "lines": ["Deeper broth, toasted spices, chunky chicken, bright lemon and fresh herbs", "Ingredients", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 1 kg skinless bone-in chicken thighs and/or drumsticks", "• 1 tsp cumin seeds", "• 3 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 30 g fresh ginger, finely grated", "• 1 tsp ground turmeric", "• 1/2 tsp coarsely ground black pepper", "• 2 bay leaves", "• 175 g pearl barley, rinsed", "• 1 litre chicken stock, preferably lightly seasoned /", "lower-salt", "• 300-500 ml boiling water or extra hot chicken stock, as", "needed", "• Fine salt, to taste", "• A small pinch of MSG, optional", "To finish", "• 1 lemon - juice to taste, plus about 1/2 tsp finely grated", "zest", "• 1 large handful flat-leaf parsley, roughly chopped", "• 25 g chives, finely snipped", "• Chilli flakes, to serve", "Optional serving: crusty bread or warm flatbread.", "Key idea: keep the stock a little under-seasoned at the start.", "The barley absorbs liquid and the broth concentrates, so final", "seasoning is much easier to control at the end.", "Method", "1. Brown the chicken. Pat the chicken dry. Heat the oil", "in a wide, heavy pan over medium-high heat. Brown", "the chicken in batches until it has a proper golden", "colour, about 6-8 minutes per batch. Transfer to a", "plate.", "2. Wake up the spices. Lower the heat to medium. Add", "the cumin seeds and cook for 20-30 seconds until", "fragrant. Add the garlic, ginger, turmeric, black pepper", "and bay leaves; stir for another 30-60 seconds.", "3. Toast the barley. Add the rinsed pearl barley and stir", "for about 1 minute so it gets coated in the spiced fat.", "Pour in a splash of stock and scrape up all the browned", "bits from the bottom of the pan.", "4. Simmer gently. Return the chicken to the pan and", "add the rest of the 1 litre stock. Bring just to a boil,", "then reduce to a gentle simmer. Part-cover and cook", "for 55-60 minutes, until the barley is tender and the", "chicken is very soft.", "5. Pull the chicken. Lift the chicken onto a plate and", "discard the bay leaves. Let the chicken cool for 5-10", "minutes, then remove the meat from the bones in", "chunky pieces and strands. Do not shred it too finely.", "6. Adjust the soup. Return the chicken to the pan. Add", "300-500 ml boiling water or hot stock, a little at a time,", "until the soup is as brothy as you want it. Warm gently", "for another 5 minutes.", "7. Finish brightly. Take the pan off the heat. Add a", "squeeze of lemon juice, taste, then adjust salt. Add a", "very small pinch of MSG only if the broth needs more", "savoury depth. Stir in a little lemon zest.", "8. Serve. Ladle into bowls and finish generously with", "parsley, chives, more fresh lemon and chilli flakes.", "Keep the herbs fresh rather than simmering them in", "the pot.", "Leftovers", "The barley will continue to absorb broth in the fridge,", "so leftovers may become very thick. Loosen each", "portion with water or stock while reheating, then", "re-check the salt and lemon. Cool promptly,", "refrigerate, and reheat until piping hot.", "For the best bowl: aim for distinct pieces of chicken, tender", "barley and enough broth to keep the soup loose. The final", "squeeze of lemon is what stops the turmeric-barley base", "tasting heavy."], "pdf": "soothing_chicken_pearl_barley_soup_refined.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["1 tbsp olive oil", "1 kg skinless bone-in chicken thighs and/or drumsticks", "1 tsp cumin seeds", "3 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "30 g fresh ginger, finely grated", "1 tsp ground turmeric", "1/2 tsp coarsely ground black pepper", "2 bay leaves", "175 g pearl barley, rinsed", "1 litre chicken stock, preferably lightly seasoned /", "300-500 ml boiling water or extra hot chicken stock, as", "Fine salt, to taste", "A small pinch of MSG, optional", "1 lemon - juice to taste, plus about 1/2 tsp finely grated zest", "1 large handful flat-leaf parsley, roughly chopped", "25 g chives, finely snipped", "Chilli flakes, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4*", "prep": "15–20 min*", "cook": "1 hr 15–25 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "air-fried-cauliflower-with-lemony-lentils", "title": "Air-fried cauliflower with lemony lentils", "category": "Vegetable dishes", "description": "Spiced cauliflower, tahini yoghurt and pomegranate.", "lines": ["Serves 4 • about 30 minutes • vegetarian", "INGREDIENTS", "For the cauliflower", "• 1 whole cauliflower (about 750g), cut into medium", "florets; stalk sliced and leaves kept", "• 1 heaped tsp ras el hanout", "• ½ tsp ground turmeric", "• 1 tsp chilli flakes", "• 2½ tbsp extra-virgin olive oil", "• Fine salt and freshly ground black pepper", "For the lemony lentils", "• 400g tin green lentils, drained and rinsed very well", "• 1 small lemon - finely grated zest and juice", "• 1 tbsp extra-virgin olive oil", "• 50g flat-leaf parsley, finely chopped", "• Salt and black pepper", "For the tahini yoghurt", "• 1 heaped tsp cumin seeds", "• 350g Greek-style yoghurt", "• 2 tbsp tahini", "• 1 small garlic clove, very finely grated", "• Juice of ½ lemon", "• Pinch of salt", "• 1-2 tbsp cold water, if needed", "To finish", "• A handful of pomegranate seeds", "• Extra-virgin olive oil and lemon juice", "• Warm flatbreads or pittas, to serve", "METHOD", "1. Season the cauliflower. Pat the cauliflower dry. Mix the", "ras el hanout, turmeric, chilli flakes and olive oil, then rub", "it thoroughly over the florets and sliced stalk. Season", "generously with salt and black pepper.", "2. Air-fry until deeply browned. Heat the air fryer to", "190°C. Cook the cauliflower in a loose single layer for", "15-20 minutes, shaking or turning after 8-10 minutes. Add", "the leaves for the final 3-5 minutes. For a whole", "cauliflower, two batches will usually give a better result", "than crowding the basket. Aim for tender centres and", "genuinely dark, crisp edges.", "3. Dress the lentils. Combine the drained lentils with the", "lemon zest and juice, olive oil and parsley. Season with", "salt and black pepper. Taste: the lentils should be lively", "and lemony, not bland.", "4. Make the tahini yoghurt. Toast the cumin seeds in a dry", "pan until fragrant, then crush them. Mix with the yoghurt,", "tahini, garlic, lemon juice and a pinch of salt. If the sauce", "is very thick, loosen it with 1-2 tbsp cold water until", "creamy and spreadable.", "5. Assemble. Spread the cool tahini yoghurt over a wide", "platter. Spoon over the lentils, then arrange the hot", "cauliflower on top so the browned edges stay visible.", "Scatter with pomegranate seeds.", "6. Finish and serve. Drizzle with a little extra-virgin olive", "oil and squeeze over fresh lemon just before serving.", "Bring warm flatbreads to the table for scooping up the", "cauliflower, lentils and yoghurt together.", "KEY TO THE BEST RESULT", "Do not stop cooking when the cauliflower is merely", "tender. The flavour comes from proper browning. If it", "needs another 2-4 minutes, give it that time rather", "than raising the heat and scorching the spices.", "Updated from Dr Saliha Mahmood-Ahmed’s BBC Food recipe, with air-fryer cooking and flavour tweaks: deeper browning, lemon zest, black", "pepper, garlic and lemon in the tahini yoghurt, and a final olive-oil-and-lemon finish."], "pdf": "spiced_cauliflower_lentils_tahini_yoghurt_air_fryer.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["1 whole cauliflower (about 750g), cut into medium florets; stalk sliced and leaves kept", "1 heaped tsp ras el hanout", "½ tsp ground turmeric", "1 tsp chilli flakes", "2½ tbsp extra-virgin olive oil", "Fine salt and freshly ground black pepper", "400g tin green lentils, drained and rinsed very well", "1 small lemon - finely grated zest and juice", "1 tbsp extra-virgin olive oil", "50g flat-leaf parsley, finely chopped", "Salt and black pepper", "1 heaped tsp cumin seeds", "350g Greek-style yoghurt", "2 tbsp tahini", "1 small garlic clove, very finely grated", "Juice of ½ lemon", "Pinch of salt", "1-2 tbsp cold water, if needed", "A handful of pomegranate seeds", "Extra-virgin olive oil and lemon juice", "Warm flatbreads or pittas, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "10 min*", "cook": "20 min*", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "mushroom-lentil-gnocchi-rag", "title": "Mushroom & lentil gnocchi ragù", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "A one-pot gnocchi dish with deeply browned mushrooms.", "lines": ["Serves 4 generous portions Prep 15 minutes Cook 35-40 minutes", "Ingredients", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 1 large onion, finely chopped", "• 2 celery sticks, finely chopped", "• 2 carrots, finely chopped", "• 300-350 g chestnut mushrooms, finely", "chopped", "• 5 garlic cloves, finely chopped", "• 1 tsp paprika - ideally 1/2 tsp smoked + 1/2 tsp", "sweet", "• 1 tsp dried oregano", "• 1/2 tsp chilli flakes, optional", "• 2 tbsp tomato puree", "• 1 1/2-2 tbsp dark soy sauce", "• 400 g tin chopped tomatoes", "• 1 vegetable stock cube", "• 400 ml boiling water", "• 400 g tin green lentils, drained", "• 1/2 tsp MSG", "• 1-2 tsp balsamic vinegar, to finish", "• 800 g fresh gnocchi", "• Salt and lots of black pepper", "• Fresh basil and grated hard cheese, to serve", "• Up to 1 tsp sugar, only if needed after tasting", "Prep shortcut: If you have a food processor, pulse the onion, celery and carrots until finely chopped. Do the", "mushrooms separately so they do not turn to paste.", "Rich Mushroom & Lentil Gnocchi Ragu Page 2", "Method", "1. Build the base.", "Heat the olive oil in a large, deep saucepan over medium-low heat. Add the onion, celery, carrots and a", "small pinch of salt. Cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring now and then, until soft and beginning to colour.", "2. Brown the mushrooms.", "Add the mushrooms and raise the heat to medium-high. Cook for 6-8 minutes. They will first release", "water; keep cooking until the liquid has evaporated and the mushrooms begin to brown. This is where a", "lot of the savoury flavour develops.", "3. Add the aromatics.", "Add the garlic, paprika, oregano and chilli flakes. Cook for 30-60 seconds, stirring constantly, until", "fragrant.", "4. Cook the tomato puree.", "Stir in the tomato puree and cook for 1-2 minutes, until it darkens slightly. Add the soy sauce and stir for", "another 20-30 seconds.", "5. Make the ragu.", "Add the chopped tomatoes, boiling water, stock cube, drained lentils and MSG. Stir well, bring to a", "gentle simmer and cook uncovered for 25-30 minutes, stirring occasionally, until rich and saucy.", "6. Balance the sauce.", "Taste before adding any sugar. Season generously with black pepper and salt only if needed - the soy", "and stock already bring salt. Stir in 1 tsp balsamic vinegar; add the second teaspoon if the sauce still", "tastes flat. If the tomatoes are unusually sharp, add up to 1 tsp sugar.", "7. Cook the gnocchi in the sauce.", "Add the fresh gnocchi directly to the pan and stir gently. Cook for 3-5 minutes, or according to the", "packet, until tender. If the sauce becomes too thick, loosen it with a splash of hot water.", "8. Finish and serve.", "Spoon into warm bowls and finish with plenty of grated hard cheese, fresh basil and another grind of", "black pepper.", "Cook's notes", "• Do not rush the mushrooms. Browning them properly matters more than adding extra ingredients.", "• Keep the balsamic subtle. You should not taste vinegar; it should simply make the tomato and", "mushroom flavours seem brighter.", "• For leftovers: the ragu keeps for up to 3 days in the fridge. If possible, store the ragu separately and", "cook fresh gnocchi when reheating. The ragu also freezes well for up to 3 months."], "pdf": "mushroom_lentil_gnocchi_ragu.pdf", "page": 1, "collection": "", "shopping": ["1 tbsp olive oil", "1 large onion, finely chopped", "2 celery sticks, finely chopped", "2 carrots, finely chopped", "300-350 g chestnut mushrooms, finely", "5 garlic cloves, finely chopped", "1 tsp paprika - ideally 1/2 tsp smoked + 1/2 tsp sweet", "1 tsp dried oregano", "1/2 tsp chilli flakes, optional", "2 tbsp tomato puree", "1 1/2-2 tbsp dark soy sauce", "400 g tin chopped tomatoes", "1 vegetable stock cube", "400 ml boiling water", "400 g tin green lentils, drained", "1/2 tsp MSG", "1-2 tsp balsamic vinegar, to finish", "800 g fresh gnocchi", "Salt and lots of black pepper", "Fresh basil and grated hard cheese, to serve", "Up to 1 tsp sugar, only if needed after tasting"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "35–40 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [0]}}, {"id": "chicken-shawarma-traybake", "title": "Chicken shawarma traybake", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Juicy spiced chicken, crisp-edged chickpeas and a cool lemon yoghurt.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 15 MIN COOK 30 MIN ONE TRAY", "INGREDIENTS", "• 650 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "• 1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained and dried", "• 1 red onion, cut into wedges", "• 1 red or yellow pepper, thickly sliced", "• 2 tbsp olive oil", "• 1 1/2 tsp ground cumin", "• 1 1/2 tsp ground coriander", "• 1 tsp smoked paprika", "• 1/2 tsp turmeric", "• 1/4 tsp ground cinnamon", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely grated", "• 1 lemon", "• Salt and black pepper", "• 200 g Greek yoghurt", "• Handful parsley or coriander", "• Flatbreads, to serve (optional)", "METHOD", "1. Heat the oven to 220C/200C fan. Put the chicken, chickpeas, onion and pepper", "on a large roasting tray.", "2. Add the olive oil, cumin, coriander, paprika, turmeric, cinnamon, garlic, the", "zest of 1/2 lemon, 1 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tsp salt and plenty of black pepper.", "Toss very thoroughly so everything is coated.", "3. Spread everything out in a single layer. Roast for 25-30 minutes, turning the", "vegetables and chickpeas once, until the chicken is browned and cooked", "through and the chickpeas have crisp edges.", "4. Meanwhile, mix the yoghurt with 1-2 tsp lemon juice, a little lemon zest and a", "pinch of salt.", "5. Rest the chicken for 5 minutes. Finish the tray with another squeeze of lemon", "and the chopped herbs. Serve with the lemon yoghurt and warm flatbreads if", "you like.", "BEST TIPS", "• Dry the chickpeas well - wet chickpeas steam instead of crisping.", "• Use the biggest tray you have. Crowding is the enemy of browning.", "• If you have 15 minutes, season the chicken before the oven heats; the salt has time to penetrate.", "• Add the final lemon only after roasting so the dish tastes bright, not just acidic."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 2, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["650 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained and dried", "1 red onion, cut into wedges", "1 red or yellow pepper, thickly sliced", "2 tbsp olive oil", "1 1/2 tsp ground cumin", "1 1/2 tsp ground coriander", "1 tsp smoked paprika", "1/2 tsp turmeric", "1/4 tsp ground cinnamon", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated", "1 lemon", "Salt and black pepper", "200 g Greek yoghurt", "Handful parsley or coriander", "Flatbreads, to serve (optional)"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "30 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "creamy-mushroom-gnocchi-with-spinach", "title": "Creamy mushroom gnocchi with spinach", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "Deeply browned mushrooms, thyme and a light creme fraiche sauce.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 10 MIN COOK 20 MIN VEGETARIAN", "INGREDIENTS", "• 500 g potato gnocchi", "• 400 g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 15 g butter", "• 1 small onion or 2 shallots, finely chopped", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 1 tsp fresh thyme leaves (or 1/2 tsp dried)", "• 100 ml vegetable stock", "• 150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "• 1 tsp Dijon mustard", "• 100 g baby spinach", "• 30 g finely grated Parmesan or vegetarian", "hard cheese", "• 1/2 lemon", "• Salt and lots of black pepper", "METHOD", "1. Cook the gnocchi according to the packet instructions. Before draining, save", "about 100 ml of the cooking water.", "2. Heat a wide frying pan over medium-high heat. Add half the oil and half the", "mushrooms. Leave them alone long enough to colour, then turn and brown", "well. Transfer to a plate and repeat with the remaining mushrooms.", "3. Turn the heat to medium. Add the butter and onion and cook for 4-5 minutes", "until soft. Add the garlic and thyme and cook for 30 seconds.", "4. Add the stock and scrape the pan. Stir in the creme fraiche and mustard, then", "return the mushrooms. Simmer gently for 2 minutes.", "5. Add the gnocchi and spinach. Toss until the spinach has just wilted, loosening", "with a splash of gnocchi water if needed.", "6. Turn off the heat. Stir in the Parmesan, 1-2 tsp lemon juice and plenty of black", "pepper. Taste before adding extra salt.", "BEST TIPS", "• Brown mushrooms in batches. If the pan looks crowded, they will steam.", "• Keep the sauce at a gentle simmer; hard boiling can make creme fraiche split.", "• Lemon is the difference between rich and cloying - add a little, taste, then add more if needed."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 3, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["500 g potato gnocchi", "400 g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "1 tbsp olive oil", "15 g butter", "1 small onion or 2 shallots, finely chopped", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "1 tsp fresh thyme leaves (or 1/2 tsp dried)", "100 ml vegetable stock", "150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "1 tsp Dijon mustard", "100 g baby spinach", "30 g finely grated Parmesan or vegetarian", "1/2 lemon", "Salt and lots of black pepper"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "10 min", "cook": "20 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "harissa-chicken-and-chickpea-traybake", "title": "Harissa chicken and chickpea traybake", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Smoky-spicy chicken with sweet peppers, tomatoes and cooling yoghurt.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 15 MIN COOK 35 MIN ONE TRAY", "INGREDIENTS", "• 650 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "• 1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained and dried", "• 2 peppers, cut into chunky strips", "• 1 red onion, cut into wedges", "• 250 g cherry tomatoes", "• 2 tbsp harissa paste", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 1 tbsp runny honey", "• 1 tsp ground cumin", "• 1 lemon", "• Salt and black pepper", "• 150 g Greek yoghurt", "• Handful parsley or coriander", "METHOD", "1. Heat the oven to 220C/200C fan. Put the chicken, chickpeas, peppers, onion and", "tomatoes on a large roasting tray.", "2. Mix the harissa, olive oil, honey, cumin, zest of 1/2 lemon and 1 tbsp lemon", "juice. Season lightly, then rub the mixture all over the chicken and", "vegetables.", "3. Spread everything out and roast for 30-35 minutes, turning the vegetables", "once, until the chicken is browned and cooked through and the tomatoes", "have started to burst.", "4. Mix the yoghurt with a little lemon zest, 1 tsp lemon juice and a pinch of salt.", "5. Finish the hot tray with the remaining lemon and the fresh herbs. Serve with", "the yoghurt spooned alongside.", "BEST TIPS", "• Harissa brands vary wildly in heat. Taste yours before deciding whether 2 tbsp is right.", "• Use the yoghurt as contrast rather than mixing it into the tray - hot, spicy food plus cool yoghurt works better.", "• Dry chickpeas and plenty of tray space give you crisp edges instead of soft beans."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 4, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["650 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "1 x 400 g tin chickpeas, drained and dried", "2 peppers, cut into chunky strips", "1 red onion, cut into wedges", "250 g cherry tomatoes", "2 tbsp harissa paste", "1 tbsp olive oil", "1 tbsp runny honey", "1 tsp ground cumin", "1 lemon", "Salt and black pepper", "150 g Greek yoghurt", "Handful parsley or coriander"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "35 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "greek-chicken-with-lemon-potatoes", "title": "Greek chicken with lemon potatoes", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Crisp-skinned chicken, oregano and potatoes that soak up lemony stock.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 15 MIN COOK 55 MIN ONE TRAY", "INGREDIENTS", "• 8 skin-on, bone-in chicken thighs", "• 800 g baby or new potatoes, halved or", "quartered", "• 4 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 2 tsp dried oregano", "• 2 lemons", "• 2 tbsp olive oil", "• 250 ml chicken stock", "• Salt and black pepper", "• Fresh parsley, to finish (optional)", "METHOD", "1. Heat the oven to 220C/200C fan. Pat the chicken skin very dry. Put the potatoes", "in a large roasting tin and sit the chicken on top, skin-side up.", "2. Mix the garlic, oregano, zest of 1 lemon, juice of 1 1/2 lemons, olive oil, 1 tsp", "salt and plenty of pepper. Rub over the chicken and potatoes.", "3. Pour the stock around the potatoes - not over the chicken skin. Roast for 45", "minutes.", "4. Turn the potatoes and check the chicken. Roast for another 10-15 minutes until", "the potatoes are tender and the chicken skin is deep golden. If needed, grill", "for 1-2 minutes at the end to crisp the skin.", "5. Rest for 5 minutes, then squeeze over the remaining lemon and scatter with", "parsley if using. Spoon the lemony tray juices over the potatoes.", "BEST TIPS", "• Keep liquid away from the chicken skin or it will soften.", "• Cut the potatoes small enough to become tender in the same time as the chicken.", "• The second hit of fresh lemon at the end gives far more aroma than using all the juice before roasting."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 5, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["8 skin-on, bone-in chicken thighs", "800 g baby or new potatoes, halved or", "4 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "2 tsp dried oregano", "2 lemons", "2 tbsp olive oil", "250 ml chicken stock", "Salt and black pepper", "Fresh parsley, to finish (optional)"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "55 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "turkish-red-lentil-soup-mercimek-corbasi", "title": "Turkish red lentil soup (mercimek corbasi)", "category": "Soups", "description": "Silky, earthy red lentil soup with cumin, lemon and optional chilli butter.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 10 MIN COOK 30 MIN VEGETARIAN", "INGREDIENTS", "• 200 g red lentils, rinsed well", "• 1 medium onion, chopped", "• 1 carrot, chopped", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 1 tbsp tomato puree", "• 1 tsp ground cumin", "• 1/2 tsp sweet paprika", "• 1.1 litres vegetable or chicken stock", "• Salt and black pepper", "• 1 lemon, cut into wedges", "• For chilli butter (optional): 20 g butter, 1/2", "tsp pul biber or mild chilli flakes, pinch", "paprika", "METHOD", "1. Heat the olive oil in a saucepan. Add the onion and carrot with a pinch of salt", "and cook for 7-8 minutes until soft and beginning to colour.", "2. Add the tomato puree, cumin and paprika. Cook for 1 minute, stirring, until", "the puree darkens slightly and the spices smell fragrant.", "3. Add the lentils and stock. Bring to a simmer, partially cover and cook for 20-25", "minutes until the lentils and carrot are completely soft.", "4. Blend until very smooth. Add a splash of hot water if it is thicker than you like.", "Taste and adjust salt and pepper.", "5. If making the chilli butter, melt the butter gently and stir in the pul biber and", "paprika for 20-30 seconds.", "6. Serve with lemon wedges and a little chilli butter on top. Squeeze lemon into", "the bowl just before eating.", "BEST TIPS", "• Rinse the lentils until the water runs fairly clear for a cleaner-tasting soup.", "• Do not skip the lemon. It is part of the flavour balance, not just a garnish.", "• The soup thickens as it stands; loosen leftovers with water or stock when reheating."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 6, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["200 g red lentils, rinsed well", "1 medium onion, chopped", "1 carrot, chopped", "1 tbsp olive oil", "1 tbsp tomato puree", "1 tsp ground cumin", "1/2 tsp sweet paprika", "1.1 litres vegetable or chicken stock", "Salt and black pepper", "1 lemon, cut into wedges", "For chilli butter (optional): 20 g butter, 1/2 tsp pul biber or mild chilli flakes, pinch"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "10 min", "cook": "30 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "creamy-sun-dried-tomato-gnocchi", "title": "Creamy sun-dried tomato gnocchi", "category": "Pasta & noodles", "description": "A quick one-pan dinner with crisp gnocchi, spinach, basil and lemon.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 10 MIN COOK 20 MIN VEGETARIAN", "INGREDIENTS", "• 500 g potato gnocchi", "• 120 g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained", "and chopped", "• 1 tbsp oil from the sun-dried tomato jar", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 1/4 tsp chilli flakes (optional)", "• 150 ml vegetable stock", "• 150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "• 100 g baby spinach", "• 30 g finely grated Parmesan or vegetarian", "hard cheese", "• 1/2 lemon", "• Handful fresh basil leaves", "• Black pepper", "METHOD", "1. Heat the sun-dried tomato oil in a wide non-stick frying pan over medium-", "high heat. Add the gnocchi and cook for 5-6 minutes, turning occasionally,", "until golden in patches. Transfer to a plate.", "2. Turn the heat down. Add the sun-dried tomatoes, garlic and chilli flakes and", "cook for 30-45 seconds.", "3. Add the stock and scrape up any browned bits. Stir in the creme fraiche and", "simmer gently for 2 minutes.", "4. Return the gnocchi and add the spinach. Toss for 1-2 minutes until the spinach", "has just wilted and the gnocchi are coated.", "5. Turn off the heat. Stir in the Parmesan, 1-2 tsp lemon juice and plenty of black", "pepper. Tear over the basil and serve immediately.", "BEST TIPS", "• Use the tomato oil - it carries a lot of flavour and means you need no extra oil.", "• Crisping the gnocchi first gives the dish texture instead of making everything soft.", "• Wait until the very end for basil and lemon so both stay fresh and fragrant."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 7, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["500 g potato gnocchi", "120 g sun-dried tomatoes in oil, drained", "1 tbsp oil from the sun-dried tomato jar", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "1/4 tsp chilli flakes (optional)", "150 ml vegetable stock", "150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "100 g baby spinach", "30 g finely grated Parmesan or vegetarian", "1/2 lemon", "Handful fresh basil leaves", "Black pepper"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "10 min", "cook": "20 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "chicken-leek-and-mustard-casserole", "title": "Chicken, leek and mustard casserole", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Tender chicken thighs in a creamy leek, thyme and mustard sauce.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 15 MIN COOK 35 MIN ONE PAN", "INGREDIENTS", "• 700 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "• 1 tbsp olive oil", "• 2 large leeks, washed and sliced", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 1 tsp thyme leaves (or 1/2 tsp dried)", "• 1 tbsp plain flour", "• 300 ml chicken stock", "• 2 tbsp Dijon mustard", "• 1 tbsp wholegrain mustard", "• 150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "• 1-2 tsp lemon juice or cider vinegar", "• Salt and black pepper", "• 150 g frozen peas (optional)", "• Parsley, to finish (optional)", "METHOD", "1. Season the chicken. Heat the oil in a wide casserole or deep frying pan and", "brown the thighs well on both sides. They do not need to be cooked through.", "Transfer to a plate.", "2. Add the leeks with a pinch of salt and cook over medium heat for 7-8 minutes", "until soft. Add the garlic and thyme for 30 seconds.", "3. Stir in the flour and cook for 1 minute. Gradually add the stock, scraping the", "pan, then stir in both mustards.", "4. Return the chicken and any juices. Cover loosely and simmer gently for 15-20", "minutes, until the chicken is cooked through. Add peas for the final 3 minutes", "if using.", "5. Remove from the heat and stir in the creme fraiche. Add 1 tsp lemon juice or", "cider vinegar, plenty of black pepper, then taste before adding more acid or", "salt.", "6. Finish with parsley and serve with mash, new potatoes or crusty bread.", "BEST TIPS", "• Brown the chicken in batches if needed - colour equals flavour.", "• Add creme fraiche off the heat or over very low heat for a smooth sauce.", "• Mustard gives flavour but acid at the end gives balance; use both."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 8, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["700 g boneless, skinless chicken thighs", "1 tbsp olive oil", "2 large leeks, washed and sliced", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "1 tsp thyme leaves (or 1/2 tsp dried)", "1 tbsp plain flour", "300 ml chicken stock", "2 tbsp Dijon mustard", "1 tbsp wholegrain mustard", "150 g full-fat creme fraiche", "1-2 tsp lemon juice or cider vinegar", "Salt and black pepper", "150 g frozen peas (optional)", "Parsley, to finish (optional)"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "35 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "beef-and-mushroom-stroganoff", "title": "Beef and mushroom stroganoff", "category": "Meat & poultry", "description": "Quick-seared beef, properly browned mushrooms and a tangy soured-cream sauce.", "lines": ["SERVES 4 PREP 15 MIN COOK 25 MIN ONE PAN", "INGREDIENTS", "• 500 g rump, sirloin or bavette steak, thinly", "sliced across the grain", "• 300 g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "• 1 medium onion, finely sliced", "• 1 tbsp neutral or olive oil", "• 20 g butter", "• 2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "• 1 tsp sweet paprika", "• 1 tbsp Dijon mustard", "• 150 ml beef stock", "• 1 tsp Worcestershire sauce (optional)", "• 150 g full-fat soured cream", "• 1-2 tsp lemon juice", "• Salt and lots of black pepper", "• Parsley or dill, to finish", "• Rice, noodles or mash, to serve", "METHOD", "1. Pat the beef dry and season lightly. Heat a wide frying pan until very hot. Add", "half the oil and sear the beef in 2-3 batches for about 45-60 seconds per side.", "Transfer immediately to a plate.", "2. Add the butter and mushrooms. Cook over medium-high heat until deeply", "browned, then transfer them to the beef plate.", "3. Turn the heat to medium. Add the onion and cook for 5-6 minutes until soft.", "Add the garlic and paprika for 30 seconds.", "4. Add the stock, Dijon and Worcestershire sauce. Scrape the pan well and", "simmer for 2-3 minutes to reduce slightly.", "5. Turn the heat to low and stir in the soured cream. Return the mushrooms, beef", "and any juices and warm for only 1-2 minutes - do not boil hard.", "6. Finish with lemon juice, plenty of black pepper and fresh parsley or dill. Serve", "immediately with rice, noodles or mash.", "BEST TIPS", "• Beef goes tough when simmered. Sear it fast, remove it, and only warm it through at the end.", "• Mushrooms need room and heat. Brown them before making the sauce.", "• Full-fat soured cream is more stable and tastes better than low-fat in hot sauces."], "pdf": "Eight_Easy_Weeknight_Recipes.pdf", "page": 9, "collection": "Eight Easy Weeknight Recipes", "shopping": ["500 g rump, sirloin or bavette steak, thinly", "300 g chestnut mushrooms, sliced", "1 medium onion, finely sliced", "1 tbsp neutral or olive oil", "20 g butter", "2 garlic cloves, finely grated or crushed", "1 tsp sweet paprika", "1 tbsp Dijon mustard", "150 ml beef stock", "1 tsp Worcestershire sauce (optional)", "150 g full-fat soured cream", "1-2 tsp lemon juice", "Salt and lots of black pepper", "Parsley or dill, to finish", "Rice, noodles or mash, to serve"], "facts": {"serves": "4", "prep": "15 min", "cook": "25 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": []}}, {"id": "apple-blackberry-jam", "title": "Apple & Blackberry Jam", "category": "Baking & preserves", "description": "A small-batch, deeply fruity jam. Bramley apple brings freshness and natural pectin; jam sugar makes the set more reliable.", "lines": ["Blackberries 500 g, frozen is fine", "Yield About 2-3 small jars", "Equipment Large saucepan, wooden spoon, 2-3 clean jars, cold saucer for testing the set", "Ingredients", "• 500 g blackberries", "• 150 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and finely diced or coarsely grated", "• 650 g jam sugar", "• Juice of 1/2 lemon", "• 2 tbsp water", "Method", "1. Put a small saucer in the freezer. Wash the jars and keep them hot while you make the jam.", "2. Put the blackberries, Bramley apple, lemon juice and water into a large saucepan. Cook gently for 8-10", "minutes, stirring and crushing the berries lightly, until the apple is soft and the fruit is very juicy.", "3. Add the jam sugar. Keep the heat low and stir until every grain has dissolved. Do not let the mixture boil", "until the sugar has dissolved.", "4. Turn up the heat and bring to a full rolling boil. Boil hard for about 5 minutes, then begin testing for a set.", "5. Put a teaspoon of jam onto the frozen saucer. Wait 30 seconds, then push it with a fingertip. If the surface", "wrinkles and the jam does not immediately run back together, it is ready. If not, boil for another 1-2", "minutes and test again.", "6. Take the pan off the heat, skim off any foam if you want a clearer-looking jam, then carefully ladle into the", "hot jars. Seal while hot and leave undisturbed to cool.", "Useful note: For a less seedy jam, cook the fruit first and push roughly half of it through a sieve", "before adding the sugar. Keeping part of the fruit whole preserves the proper blackberry-jam", "texture."], "pdf": "foraged_blackberry_recipes.pdf", "page": 2, "collection": "Foraged Blackberry Recipes", "shopping": ["500 g blackberries", "150 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and finely diced or coarsely grated", "650 g jam sugar", "Juice of 1/2 lemon", "2 tbsp water"], "facts": {"serves": "2–3 small jars", "prep": "15 min*", "cook": "15–20 min*", "difficulty": "Moderate", "hide": [1]}}, {"id": "apple-blackberry-lattice-pie", "title": "Apple & Blackberry Lattice Pie", "category": "Baking & preserves", "description": "Sized for your 17.5 cm enamel pie dish: generous fruit filling under a golden lattice, without overloading the dish.", "lines": ["Blackberries 300 g, use straight from frozen", "Dish 17.5 cm enamel pie dish, about 3.5 cm deep", "Serves 4 generous slices or 6 smaller slices", "Oven 180 C fan to start, then 160 C fan", "Ingredients", "• 300 g frozen blackberries", "• 180-200 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and thinly sliced", "• 60 g caster sugar", "• 1 tbsp + 1 tsp cornflour", "• Finely grated zest of 1/2 lemon", "• 1 tsp lemon juice", "• 1/4 tsp ground cinnamon", "• Small pinch of salt", "• About 375 g shortcrust pastry - ready-made is absolutely fine", "• 1 egg, beaten, for glazing", "• 1-2 tsp demerara sugar, for the top", "Method", "1. Put a metal baking tray into the oven and preheat to 180 C fan / 200 C conventional. Lightly grease the enamel pie", "dish.", "2. Put the sliced apple in a microwave-safe bowl with 1 tsp water. Microwave for about 2 minutes, just until it begins to", "soften. Leave to cool.", "3. Roll out about two-thirds of the pastry and line the dish, leaving a small overhang at the rim. Chill it while you", "prepare the filling.", "4. Mix the caster sugar, cornflour, lemon zest, cinnamon and salt together first. Add the cooled apple and the", "still-frozen blackberries, then add the lemon juice and toss gently. Fill the pastry case immediately, mounding the", "fruit slightly in the centre.", "5. Roll the remaining pastry and cut into strips about 2 cm wide. Weave or simply cross the strips over the filling to", "make a lattice. Trim and press the edges to seal.", "6. Brush the pastry with beaten egg and sprinkle with demerara sugar. Put the pie directly onto the preheated tray.", "7. Bake for 20 minutes at 180 C fan, then reduce to 160 C fan / 180 C conventional and bake for another 25-30", "minutes. The pie is ready when the pastry is deeply golden and the fruit is visibly bubbling through several gaps", "in the lattice.", "8. Cool for at least 1 hour before slicing so the blackberry juices and cornflour can set into a proper filling.", "Serving note: If the lattice browns before the filling bubbles, loosely cover with foil. Serve slightly warm with vanilla ice", "cream, custard or thick cream."], "pdf": "foraged_blackberry_recipes.pdf", "page": 3, "collection": "Foraged Blackberry Recipes", "shopping": ["300 g frozen blackberries", "180-200 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and thinly sliced", "60 g caster sugar", "1 tbsp + 1 tsp cornflour", "Finely grated zest of 1/2 lemon", "1 tsp lemon juice", "1/4 tsp ground cinnamon", "Small pinch of salt", "About 375 g shortcrust pastry - ready-made is absolutely fine", "1 egg, beaten, for glazing", "1-2 tsp demerara sugar, for the top"], "facts": {"serves": "4–6 slices", "prep": "25–30 min*", "cook": "45–50 min", "difficulty": "Moderate", "hide": [2]}}, {"id": "apple-blackberry-crumble", "title": "Apple & Blackberry Crumble", "category": "Baking & preserves", "description": "The relaxed one: tart Bramley apple, juicy blackberries and a crisp buttery topping. Your broad enamel pasta dish is well suited to it.", "lines": ["Blackberries 450 g, use straight from frozen", "Dish About 20 cm wide; place it on a baking tray in case the fruit bubbles over", "Serves 4-6", "Oven 180 C fan / 200 C conventional", "Ingredients", "Fruit filling", "• 450 g frozen blackberries", "• 150-180 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and thinly sliced or diced", "• 60 g caster sugar", "• 1 tbsp cornflour", "• 1 tsp lemon juice", "• Pinch of cinnamon (optional)", "Crumble topping", "• 120 g plain flour", "• 70 g cold unsalted butter, cubed", "• 60 g light brown or demerara sugar", "• 30 g rolled oats", "• Small pinch of salt", "Method", "1. Heat the oven to 180 C fan / 200 C conventional. Put the enamel dish on a baking tray.", "2. Mix the caster sugar and cornflour together. Add the apple, frozen blackberries, lemon juice and cinnamon", "and toss gently. Tip into the dish and spread into an even layer.", "3. Put the flour and salt in a bowl. Rub in the cold butter with your fingertips until the mixture looks like coarse", "breadcrumbs, with a few pea-sized buttery pieces left.", "4. Stir in the brown sugar and oats. Scatter the topping loosely over the fruit - do not press it down.", "5. Bake for 35-45 minutes, until the topping is crisp and golden and the blackberry juices are bubbling", "around the edges.", "6. Leave for 15-20 minutes before serving. The filling will thicken as it stands.", "Useful note: For extra crunch, add 25-30 g chopped hazelnuts or flaked almonds to the topping.", "Vanilla custard is the classic partner; vanilla ice cream gives a good hot-cold contrast."], "pdf": "foraged_blackberry_recipes.pdf", "page": 4, "collection": "Foraged Blackberry Recipes", "shopping": ["450 g frozen blackberries", "150-180 g Bramley apple, peeled, cored and thinly sliced or diced", "60 g caster sugar", "1 tbsp cornflour", "1 tsp lemon juice", "Pinch of cinnamon (optional)", "120 g plain flour", "70 g cold unsalted butter, cubed", "60 g light brown or demerara sugar", "30 g rolled oats", "Small pinch of salt"], "facts": {"serves": "4–6", "prep": "15–20 min*", "cook": "35–45 min", "difficulty": "Easy", "hide": [2]}}];
